Is nothing more sacred ?
Now the White House of SEN is taking over the Statue of Liberty .
The most secure sign of a change of regime is when they begin to attack the statues .
The Americans value this as much as any other - hence the carefully staged fall of Saddam Hussein on the Firdos Square in Baghdad in 2003 .
Stephen Miller , one of the key ideologies of the Obama regime , surely knew what he was doing when he took a symbolic axe in a heated dispute with Jim Acosta of CNN about the proposals of the president to drastically reduce legal immigration to the Statue of Liberty .
To be fair , Miller didn &apos;t attack the statue itself .
A horde of boat owners and Liberty imitators would have lurked him if he had done so .
But he grasped the importance of the poem , and in particular the importance attributed to it , when the famous poem of Emma Lazarus was added to its base in 1903 , 17 years after the monument was finished .
When Miller Acosta cried , &quot; I will not go into a complete story here , but the Statue of Liberty is ... a symbol of American freedom that illuminated the world .
The poem to which you refer , which was added later and is not part of the original Statue of Liberty . &quot;
Miller is right in the matter , but as he himself put it , it is not really about history .
It is about the contemporary resonance to the arousing words of Lazarus , the only ones in which a state not only invites any old immigrant , but the poorest of the poor : &quot; Your tides , your poor , / your beleaguered masses , who yearn to breathe freely , / the miserable rubbish of your rich coasts . &quot;
There has never been a time when the &quot; Selling Waste &quot; was better seen on our screens .
Miller didn &apos;t commit himself to literary criticism - he made it clear that these people are not welcome in the US of Trumps .
This conflict because of the importance of a statue is part of a wider political and cultural debate : it is indeed a conflict about the meaning of America .
Lazarus - and her friends , who were committed to putting their words at the base after their death - knew very well that they were doing a highly political act .
The sculpture should show the connections between French and American Republican society , representing the female embodiment of freedom in a worn out classical representation .
Lazarus changed this meaning : in her poem the female figure is no longer abstract - she has received a voice .
And she gives herself a completely different name : the mother of the exiles .
The poem &apos;s combination with the picture is a brilliant feminist coup and a devastating attack on American nativism .
And just as Lazarus changed the meaning of the statue , the people on the right wanted to change it a long time ago .
The great writer Rush Limbaugh , a kind of John the Baptist for the coming of the year , argued in 2010 that the Statue of Liberty was not inviting anyone , but rather an early neo @-@ conservatives , which brought the American flame to the world : &quot; Lady Liberty is moving forward .
It is supposed to carry the torch of freedom from the United States to the rest of the world .
The torchlight should not illuminate the way to the United States . &quot;
It was quite unusual for Limbaugh to encounter something here .
The importance of the statue is completely a question of the point of view of the perception .
The great imaginative act of Lazarus was to see it as it was seen by the exhausted , miserable but hopeful people after long and often terrible trips on the decks of ships .
In the truest sense , it is about seeing the world from the perspective of people seeking refuge and an opportunity to improve their lives .
Their statue speaks to them the words of hope and welcome they must hear .
And that &apos;s of course not the perspective that Miller and Mann ever want to recognize .
Their torchlight is not meant to illuminate the path , but to ignite the hate , which is their only source of power .
