Blocking of critics on Facebook : Do not apply for a public post if you can &apos;t afford to heat up voters .
The father of our country knew of a bad press .
Americans loved George Washington , but it took not long for the newspapers to beat him for everything , from domestic to political principles .
He criticized , safely .
But he didn &apos;t silence his critics .
For in 1783 , Washington said , &quot; Freedom of speech can be removed - and , stupid and mute , we are led to slaughter like the sheep . &quot;
This takes me to Maryland to Governor Larry Hogan , who has to work to be more like Washington .
Hogans employees have blocked and deleted the contributions of at least 450 people who have expressed their opinion on his official Facebook page .
And the American Civil Liberties Union sued him for this at the beginning of this week .
The Stab of the Governor dismissed the appeal as frivol , and the online comment was full of a reaction with &quot; Na , and ? . &quot;
&quot; It &apos;s just Facebook , &quot; many people said .
But it is important .
And it is especially important when it is a type like Hogan .
This is a Republican Governor in an overwhelming democratic state that is surprisingly popular .
He has the second highest consent of the 50 governors of the nation .
Hogan is not a reactionary heat head .
He has shown a firm hand in the leadership of his state and a strict adherence to the principles .
He was also very clever in using Facebook as a primary means to connect to his electorate and playing his hair @-@ less head for chemotherapy on his side .
Therefore , blocking people who come to the Governor &apos;s side - which is a public forum called the official one and by employees paid with public tax dollars - is unnecessary and ultimately dangerous .
In an interview with The Washington Post , Hogan &apos;s speaker Amelia Chasse defended the actions of the Governor and argued that blocking the comments was nothing more than a moderation .
But it is too easy to use the image of trolls or spammers or hate @-@ filled people who sweep the whip online .
The post talked to some of the real people who were blocked by Hogan .
And they are only real people who speak to their chosen leaders : a teacher , an entrepreneur and a pastor , not a troll .
They all said that their comments were respectful , thoughtful and not proactive .
The priest quoted the Bible in his post and appealed to Hogans Catholic faith .
Lawyer Lakshmi Sarma Ramani of Bowie , Md . , was not hate @-@ filled , but she asked about hate crimes .
&quot; I politely commented that I was disappointed that there was no reaction to hate crimes and other current news , &quot; she wrote in the comment section of the message &quot; The Post . &quot;
&quot; I don &apos;t like this idea either , if a number of people take a position on the same topic , then some tend to disregard it as a so @-@ called collective commitment and not as a large group of citizens concerned . &quot;
What the Governor &apos;s employees referred to as a &quot; concentrated spam attack , &quot; would probably be called &quot; intercession . &quot;
The Facebook era makes it easy to cut a message by simply blocking a critic or removing a negative comment .
It is much cleaner than in the past to have done the same thing , that employees were sent to collect and burn newspapers with critical articles or to arrest and silence demonstrators .
But that is what is happening in digital form .
Hogan is not the first public civil servant to be criticized for having accused Facebook and other social media .
President Obama is sued by Twitter users who were blocked by his Twitter feed .
One of the first milestones in this issue was last week in Virginia .
The Chairman of the Board of Supervisors of Loudoun County violated the First Amendment , according to U.S. District Judge James C. Cacheris in Alexandria , when she banished a voter from her Facebook page .
And in Kentucky Governor Matt Bevin ( R ) also received a visit from the ACLU for his use of Facebook and Twitter .
That should not be so heavy .
At the time of Washington , the period of affordable porn had a similar effect to the Internet .
The number of newspapers multiplied between 1776 and 1800 , and anonymous writers were alluding to his leadership .
And even then , Washington had anonymous troll .
People using pseudonyms such as &quot; Juricola , &quot; &quot; Valerius , &quot; &quot; Belisarius &quot; and &quot; Portius &quot; wrote all letters to newspapers that made Washington &apos;s decision .
Petitions criticizing his views of the Amity Treaty , the trade and the shipping with the United Kingdom overwhelmed his office , according to the historical documents collected by the online papers of the George Washington Project .
But he didn &apos;t silence her .
Freedom of speech , disagreement and discourse are the core of our nation .
And true leadership means accepting this .
