Poverty and Death in the Indonesian Golden Land
When Bardina Degei cooks the dinner , she does not use a stove .
She even rarely uses a pot .
In her wooden house in Enarotali , the capital of the Paniai Regency in the troubled Indonesian province of Papua , the housewife normally places a sweet potato - locally called &quot; nota &quot; - directly in the fireplace .
After half an hour , the cured tubing is removed and swallowed with eager , unwashed hands .
Degei is on the mud floor - she has no furniture - where she also performs her daily work , such as washing clothes with dirty water from the nearby swamp .
A bucket in a rooftop room serves as a latrine .
The youngest of the four wives of her husband was not assigned any fields .
Polygamy is a common procedure .
Of course , it can be dangerous to work late : most village men are unemployed , and many are drinking hard , and there are soldiers here .
&quot; Nobody dares to go around the village after 5 pm , &quot; she says .
It is a rare insight into the daily life in the highlands of Papua , a former Dutch colony , which was taken in 1969 after a controversial referendum in Indonesia , when only 1,026 elders were to vote through a public elevation before the occupation troops .
An existing movement that was pushing for independence against the Dutch dominion quickly turned against the Jakarta government , which maintains control over the region and excludes foreign journalists or legal observers from it .
In 2003 , the province was officially divided into Papua and West Papua , with the independent part of Papua New Guinea being the eastern part of the island .
Enarotali is as remote as it is deserted , with a 90 @-@ minute flight from the provincial capital Jayapura to Nabire and a 5 @-@ hour morning car rental .
There is no public transport .
The town with about 19.000 inhabitants is made up of wooden houses surrounded by bamboo fences and corrugated roofs , which have been transformed by rust into different yellow @-@ brown tones .
Very few Indonesians have travelled to this place , let alone journalists and virtually no foreigners .
Before Christian missionaries arrived , Mee Pago Papuans worshipped a god called Uga Tamee .
There were other changes .
&quot; We were not accustomed to wear these clothes , &quot; Degei says and points to their lively , hand @-@ woven turban , their dark shirt and their bright rock .
&quot; In the past , we had only leaves on our bodies . &quot;
Papua is Indonesia &apos;s poorest province , with 28 % of people living below the poverty line and some of the worst child mortality and literacy rates in Asia .
But it is also Indonesia &apos;s gold country .
The largest and most profitable gold mine in the world , Grasberg , owned by the Phoenix Freeport McMoran , is only 60 miles from Paniai , a high @-@ land province in the size of New Jersey with 153,000 inhabitants .
In 2015 alone , Freeport has dismantled $ 3.1 billion in gold and copper .
In addition , Papua has a wood resources worth an estimated USD 78 billion .
However , these riches are a source of misery for the Papua , which ensures that Indonesia &apos;s powerful military maintains a suffocating presence .
A 2005 investigation in the New York Times reported that Freeport paid local military and units between 1998 and 2004 almost $ 20 million , including up to $ 150,000 to a single officer .
Papuan demands for greater autonomy threaten these golden goodies and are treated without mercy .
According to activists , more than 500,000 Papua have been killed and thousands have been raped , tortured and imprisoned by the Indonesian military since 1969 .
In the 1970 &apos;s mass deaths in Papua &apos;s tribal highlands became genocide in accordance with the Asia Human Rights Commission .
In the region alone , the Indonesian police arrested more than 3,900 peaceful demonstrators last year .
&quot; We Will Loss &quot; is the phrase in a report by the archdiocese of Brisbane from 2016 , which contains reports of the atrocities of the prior year , such as unlawful executions and torture - rape and electric strikes are especially popular after another report - and the brutal precipitation of peaceful demonstrations .
&quot; It is difficult to count the number of victims , as every week events happen , &quot; says Andreas Harsono , Indonesia researchers for Human Rights Watch .
The screws have been strengthened , as Papua &apos;s resources bring in an influx of settlers from other parts of Indonesia .
The population of 3.5 million inhabitants of the province is 83 % Christian , but the demographic trend is changing as Muslim economic migrants come from the dense islands Java , Borneo , Sumatra and Sulawesi .
Javanese Warning Cantins Sell fried chicken and gado @-@ gado , a mixed vegetable served with peanut sauce .
Local people are fighting to keep up with this .
&quot; The migrants have begun to sell chickens and vegetables on the traditional market at a cheaper rate than the local papua , &quot; explains Abeth You , a 24 @-@ year @-@ old panic native who went to work in the provincial capital Jayapura .
&quot; It has tasted the local Papua - the Mama @-@ Mama &#91; the women &#93; of Papua - their market . &quot;
The Indonesian President , Joko Widodo , known as Jokowi , promised to address the inequalities and the violations of the law in Papua during his 2014 campaign .
The former carpenter provided 27 of the 29 districts of Papua - including Paniai - on the way to the Presidential Palace in Jakarta .
But in Papua , little has changed and today the locals feel cheated .
&quot; Our hearts are broken because we have voted for Jokowi in 2014 , with the expectation that he will fulfill our hopes that justice will be restored , &quot; you .
In fact , only two months after Jokowi &apos;s inauguration in October , Paniai suffered a low point .
On 7th December 2014 , a group of 11 children was out there and sang Christmas songs in front of a campfire in Enarotali , when two Indonesian soldiers on a motorcycle broke through the darkness .
The children , who were frightened , told them to turn on their headlights .
One of the soldiers took the sound of the fire and later came back with four soldiers , so the local Pastor Yavedt Tebai explained .
The soldiers who had drunk hunted and beaten the group with their rifle pistons , said victims and witnesses .
Then one of the soldiers fired in the group of children .
A child , 16 @-@ year @-@ old Yulianus Yeimo , was so hard hit that he fell into coma .
A few hours later , the building of the nearby Government Commission was set on fire and things escalated the next day .
About 1,000 young Papua men , women and children gathered in a football field in front of the local police station and the Military Command Centre to demand justice .
They performed ceremonial arches of hunting and performed the Waita dance - in the circle continuously and imitating a bird song - by the Papua Mee Pago tribe .
Some demonstrators started to spin stones at police and military posts .
When the mood was heated , the soldiers were sent a command over an internal radio : &quot; When the masses resist more than three times , they are shooting them dead , &quot; says a TIME official document that was not released to the local media .
Yeremias Kayame , 56 , the head of the Kego Koto quarter of Enarotali , saw the threat and called for peace and the crowd to return home .
Nobody was in the mood to listen .
&quot; When I turned , I was shot to my left wrist , &quot; he said , &quot; TIME on the veranda of his colorful painted wooden house .
Kayame still does not know who fired , but says that the ball came from the ranks of the soldiers gathered here .
&quot; It was overcrowded , many shots were fired , &quot; he adds .
The native man Alfius Youw was met three times , so his cousin , who witnessed the shoots , was a witness .
&quot; I ran to him and investigated his body to make sure that he was , &quot; said Yohanes , who , like many Indonesians , only bears a name , the TIME in a dire mood .
&quot; I saw that he was dead ... I kissed him . &quot;
The chief inspector of the Papua police , Yotje Mende , told reporters that his officers wanted to &quot; secure &quot; their station only because of the attack .
&quot; We must defend ourselves if people threaten to kill us , &quot; the spokesman of the Papua police , Commissioner Pudjo Sulistiyo , said in 2015 .
&quot; It is about life and death . &quot;
According to Human Rights Watch , five young demonstrators were killed and many others were injured .
The news about the killing came to Jakarta only the following day &apos; filtered &apos; .
Three weeks in Jayapura , Jokowi made a passionate speech , in which he expressed sympathy for the families of the victims and swept to address the historical abuses in Papua .
&quot; I want this case to be resolved immediately , so that it won &apos;t happen in the future , &quot; he said .
In October 2016 , Minister of Security Wiranto said that he was establishing an extrajudicial mechanism to resolve historical violations of human rights .
But the excuses started almost immediately .
&quot; Most injuries occurred a long time ago .
Some were in the 90 &apos;s and early 2000 &apos;s .
The point is that we are committed to tackling these violations , but there are processes that have to be pursued , &quot; he said .
Then , Wiranto made a retreat when he spoke with TIME on 5th June in Jakarta , saying that he had no plans to establish a complaint mechanism in Papua .
Instead , &quot; everything is governed by law , &quot; he said .
Wiranto , which the UN has accused of &quot; crimes against humanity &quot; causing more than 1,000 deaths in East Timorese &apos;s blood independence vote of 1999 , said that 11 cases of human rights violations had already been resolved in Papua , including the Paniai incident .
Families of the Paniai victims received such allegations with grim disbelief .
&quot; I have been interviewed four times in the last three years , but there has been no progress , &quot; says Yohanes .
&quot; I &apos;m tired . &quot;
He says that years later he is still living in fear .
&quot; I &apos;m afraid , &quot; he says .
&quot; I am afraid to be arrested by the military , I am afraid to be shot . &quot;
His brother Yacobus repeated the view that the people in Paniai are afraid to discuss the incident .
He says that he was beaten by the military after helping to bury four of the victims .
&quot; After I had buried the corpses , the military searched for me , &quot; he says .
The shooting did not stop .
On Tuesday , the Indonesian police shoved on villagers in Paniai &apos;s neighborhood Deiyai .
During a confrontation between villagers and the manager of a construction company that refused to take an unconscious man to the hospital , one person died and 17 others were injured , including children .
The man , who was 24 years old Ravianus Douw , drowned when he was fishing in a nearby river , died on his way to the hospital .
Inspired villagers protested in front of the company &apos;s location office .
The police said that the villagers threw stones at officers who responded by firing warning shots .
But locals say that the mobile brigade ( Indonesian paramilitary police ) started to shoot at the crowd and killed a .
&quot; We were so in a state of panic , we are afraid that there will be revenge , &quot; says the 29 @-@ year @-@ old Dominggu Badii , who lived near the hospital and was a witness of the way the injured were quickly brought in here , so the TIME .
&quot; I have hired myself for two days in my house . &quot;
The Deiyai Parliament has called for the officers involved to be held to account and the police mobile brigade to be removed from the area .
Paniai was always a source of discomfort for the Indonesian government .
The lack of a meaningful development reinforces the dissatisfaction of the tribal peoples Mee , Moni , Dani and Damal , who live in the green highlands of Papua .
Many joined the &quot; Free Papua Movement &quot; ( OPM ) , the rebel army claiming to defend the rights of the Papua by launching sporadic attacks and kidnappings of Indonesian soldiers .
Some of the top OPM leaders come from Paniai , including Tadius Yogi and Daniel Yudas Kogoya .
As a reaction , thousands of people have been arrested in Paniai in the last years and arbitrarily arrested , on the excuse of &quot; securing national sovereignty . &quot;
Some never appear again .
Among the people of Papua , Paniai is known as &quot; a forgotten place , a tragic place . &quot;
Poverty reinforces dissatisfaction .
The little rice that is sold in Enarotali is too expensive for locals .
Bread is just as far out of reach .
The people here are building everything they eat : especially nota and fruits and leaflets .
Agriculture is the task of women , who can each process four or five fields of the sweet potatoes .
They retain the majority of the harvest for the family , while the rest is sold on the local market .
Ten pieces Nota cost only 10,000 Indonesian rupees ( 75 cents ) .
Over time , economic inequalities have grown between the indigenous Papua and the new migrants , who have arrived in greater numbers since the opening of new routes to the airport of Nabire .
The few jobs in the region are usually to be found in the more educated and wealthy migrants .
Papua rarely have the capital or the skills necessary to run its own companies competitively .
&quot; The young people are not interested in staying in the village ... because there are no jobs or money here , &quot; says John Gobai , Chairman of the Paniai tribal council .
Isolation keeps the eyes of the world far from Papua .
In addition , restrictions on international media coverage remain tight .
At the beginning of the year , the French journalists Franck Escudie and Basille Longchamp were deported for lack of coordination with related institutions from Papua , although they were granted rare permission to film .
According to Phelim Kine , the Deputy Director of Human Rights Watch , the promises of Jokowi &apos;s election campaign to lift the restrictions on reporting in order to promote transparency and development have not been implemented .
&quot; There are new threats to foreign journalists trying to report from Indonesia &apos;s most restless eastern provinces of Papua and West Papua : visa refusal and a black list , &quot; he said in a statement .
Lack of press control means that international pressure on the Indonesian government remains largely limited to Papua &apos;s immediate neighbors .
In March , six Pacific states - Tonga , Nauru , Palau , Tuvalu , the Marshall Islands and the Solomon Islands - called on the UN Human Rights Council to investigate the &quot; various and widespread violations &quot; in Papua , including the Paniai Shooting .
The same countries historically supported the OPM .
The speaker of the Indonesian Foreign Ministry , Arrmanatha Nasir , rejected the group &apos;s accusations and said journalists in Jakarta : &quot; In Indonesia still has a democratic system and there are free media , so it is difficult to cover the evidence of human rights cases . &quot;
People on the spot want more foreign governments to take note of this .
As an official delegation from the Netherlands , led by the Human Rights ambassador Kees Van Baar , Jayapura , visited on May 4 , the locals broke their silence and urged him to &quot; We want freedom , &quot; according to a source that also attended the meeting , but wants to remain anonymous .
Indonesia has another presidential election in 2019 , but the Papua say that they will probably not vote for Jokowi again .
&quot; Jokowi is a person with good intentions , but he is surrounded by people involved in the Paniai shots , &quot; says Gobai , Chairman of the tribal council .
He wants Jokowi to know that the Paniai people , besides being under the constant threat of a predatory military , are in poor education and health care .
Gobai says that the Paniai people , like other Papua , view their vote for Jokowi as a &quot; debt &quot; that he has to pay back .
&quot; They don &apos;t need money , they just want justice , &quot; he says .
Despite the threats and intimidations , families of the Paniai @-@ Fire victims carried out a final symbolic act of the troop : the body of a victim was buried on a site exactly opposite the police and the military station .
They know that they may never be given justice , but at least they don &apos;t leave those in charge to forget their crimes .
&quot; A member of our family was killed , &quot; says Yacobus with a singing head .
&quot; What else can we do ? &quot;
Fire capture the &apos; Torch &apos; skyscraper of Dubai for the second time .
A fire hit the streets of Dubai on Friday in one of the highest residential towers in the world , forcing hundreds of residents to flee the building , which is 337 metres high and has a 79 @-@ storey building .
It is the second fire that sweeps through the tower in just two years , which is by chance called the Torch Tower ( Fackel Tower ) .
The building was evacuated , no injuries were reported , and there was no direct word about the cause of the flame .
&quot; We have been sleeping and we have stood up to the fire alarm and to the cries of the people .
We ran down the stairs and it took about 10 minutes to get down from the 50th floor , &quot; said a resident who called his name George , Reuters .
The civil security authorities of Dubai said that the Fire Control Units extinguished the fire and cooled the building at about 4 : 00 local time ( 00.00 GMT ) .
The government said that it is working on providing accommodation for the people concerned .
The incident can revive questions about the safety of materials used in the exteriors of high @-@ rise buildings around the world .
A review by the management of the Torch building after its fire in 2015 showed that the greater part of the damage was from the lining , the exterior facade for decoration or insulation .
In the UK , the police said they believe that the system of panels for insulation and panelling at the London Grenfell Tower in June could have contributed to the rapid spread of a fire in which about 80 people died .
No Davis Cup reform : Continue Best @-@ of @-@ five .
The President of the International Tennis Association ( ITF ) has fallen short of his large Davis Cup coup at the General Assembly .
At the meeting in Ho @-@ Chi @-@ Minh City , the revolutionary proposal to play Davis Cup singles on only two winning rates was not supported by the required two @-@ thirds majority .
The five @-@ set classics , which are often very long , but also highly valued for this reason , remain the main focus of the traditional competition .
63.54 percent of the member states voted for the reform , so the goal of the ITF @-@ president , David Haggerty , was therefore only a short way to go .
&quot; We respect this decision , but are disappointed that our member nations have not adopted the entire reform package for the Davis Cup and the Federal Reserve Cup .
We need a change to ensure the long @-@ term future of these iconic and historical competitions , &quot; said Haggerty .
Work will continue to be done to improve the job application .
Not only the vote , after many objections , had already been made , the proposal to hold the final in a neutral place was already made .
As a new addition to the previous year , the finalists will have the right to host the Davis Cup and the Federal Cup in the first round of the year .
To reduce the costs for the organizers , the requirements for training places etc. will be simplified and the players will have to attend less official media appointments before the Davis Cup .
More than 1 out of 5 US travellers bring forbidden items to the aircraft , a survey reveals .
A survey of air passengers showed that more than 20 percent of the objects that were knowingly or unintentionally banned were smuggled past the control points of the US Transportation Security Administration , including at least 6 percent who entered the aircraft while they were accidentally carrying forbidden knives or other objects with blades .
Less than 1 percent claimed that they discovered late that they were mistaken with guns , ammunition or explosives .
The survey of more than 1,000 people - carried out by a jet charter service , the Stratos Jet Charters Inc . - also found that younger travellers tend to dislike the rules .
Of those surveyed , who admitted that they knowingly attempted to fly with something that was forbidden by the TSA , 19.7 percent were Millennium , compared to less than 15 percent , who were members of Generation X .
The most frequent objects that were deliberately passed over to TSA agents were food and fluids .
More than 3 percent concede that they have knowingly carried objects with blades through the security control , while 2.2 percent of the female respondents and 3.7 percent of the male respondents also confirmed that they have deliberately brought prohibited drugs to the airplane .
The company said that it asked travellers across the country to figure out how many smuggling goods accidentally brought through airport security and how many people would have done it intentionally .
It is no surprise that the people who are most likely to carry smuggled goods - knowingly or unknowingly - through the safety were the people who fly the most .
The company points out , however , that the TSA guidelines are somewhat complicated and confusing .
People are often unsure which liquids they can take with them or how much of them .
It is not surprising that the largest amount of the items seized by the TSA are prohibited liquids .
For a while people thought that the TSA could see books as a smuggling commodity .
Liquids are allowed on domestic flights , but not on international flights .
Elon Musk does not care : tens of thousands of Tesla customers cancel order .
Elektro @-@ Thousand Sassa Elon Musk is happy to announce the huge number of advance orders for his new &quot; Model 3 &quot; Tesla .
He has so far failed to allow some of the customers to cancel their non @-@ binding reservation .
In addition to the ever @-@ increasing numbers for sales increases and even more gigantic sales targets , Tesla &apos;s Head Elon Musk has for the first time betrayed a different , less brilliant figure in announcing the latest quarterly report : the one for the cancellations of the new mass market model 3 .
Musk had frequently advertised the new car in the last months with more than 500.000 orders , the delivery of which is just starting .
Now , Musk is conceiving : 63,000 of the total 518.000 pre @-@ orders have already been withdrawn .
Critics have repeatedly pointed out that the reservation is only a non @-@ binding one .
In case of cancellation , Tesla will refund the 1000 Euro ( US $ 1000 and UK $ 1000 ) &quot; reservation deposit . &quot;
Currently , 455,000 such non @-@ binding reservations are available , and Musk has granted the right to make a telephone conference with analysts .
The Tesla @-@ Chef does not believe that cancelling up to now about 12 percent of all orders could be a problem .
His argument : The challenge for the ambitious growth targets lies not in the demand , but in the production side .
Last year , Tesla delivered 84,000 vehicles .
2018 , all the models put together , are to be 500.000 , and by 2020 a million .
The demand for this is obvious .
According to Musk , there are currently 1800 reservations per day .
If you order a &quot; Model 3 &quot; today , you can expect delivery at the earliest in the second half of 2018 - if Tesla is able to drive your brand new , never @-@ before @-@ tested production lines with maximum capacity without major interruptions .
Many experts doubt that this is possible .
However , should there be a major delay , the non @-@ binding of the reservations could become a problem for Tesla .
The company can &apos;t prevent dissatisfied customers from leaving a multitude of stores .
EPO boss Pruitt withdraws due to the delay of the Obama Era &apos;s rules to reduce emissions .
One day after being sued by 15 states , Scott Pruitt , the head of the Environmental Protection Agency , returned his earlier decision to delay the implementation of the Obama era &apos;s rules to reduce the emissions of Smog @-@ causing air pollutants .
Pruitt presented the change when its agency proved to be more responsive to the needs of the environmental authorities than previous administrations .
He did not mention the appeal that had been brought against his previous position in a Federal Supreme Appeal Court .
It is a matter of a deadline that expired on October 1 for states to meet the 2015 standards for soil @-@ related ozone .
Pruitt announced in June that he would delay compliance for a year in order to give the authority more time , study the plan and avoid &quot; interfering in local decisions or hindering economic growth . &quot;
Pruitt , who was Oklahoma &apos;s public prosecutor before being appointed by President Donald brenner , was a long @-@ time , reliable opponent of stricter environmental regulations .
Since his arrival in Washington , Pruitt has repeatedly moved to block or delay regulations that the chemical and fossil fuel industries oppose .
The sudden reversal of Wednesday is the most recent legal setback for Pruit &apos;s regulatory rollback agenda .
Last month , a Federal Supreme Court in Washington ruled that the EPO administrator had exceeded his authority in an attempt to delay the implementation of an Obama administration rule that required oil and gas companies to monitor and reduce methanlecks .
In a statement published on Wednesday evening , Pruitt proposed that his turn in ozone standards simply strengthen the EPO &apos;s readiness to work with the states through the complex process of timely compliance with the new standards .
&quot; Among the previous administrations , the EPO often failed to meet the deadlines for the implementation and then waited for being accused by activists and other groups , and to agree on the date for the implementation , &quot; said Pruitt , who complained to the EPO more than a dozen times in his prior job .
&quot; We do not believe in a regulation by court proceedings , and we take deadlines seriously .
We also take the law and the authority that we have , seriously . &quot;
Nevertheless , the EPO &apos;s opinion stated that Pruitt will at some point use his &quot; power of delay and all other powers available in law &quot; to ensure that the regulations are &quot; based on a sound policy and the best available information . &quot;
The Republicans in the Congress are pushing for a more comprehensive reform of the ozone rules .
A bill , which was approved last month , is intended to delay the implementation of the 2015 rules for at least eight years .
The measure has not yet been put to the vote in the Senate .
The New York Attorney General Eric Schneiderman , who headed the Coalition of States that had sued the EPO this week , said that the Group intends to maintain the pressure of the law .
&quot; The EPO &apos;s reversal - after our complaints - is an important asset for the health and safety of these 6.7 million New Yorkers and the more than 115 million Americans who are directly affected by the smog that is pouring into their communities , &quot; Schneiderman said .
New York joined California , Connecticut , Delaware , Illinois , Iowa , Maine , Massachusetts , Minnesota , New Mexico , Oregon , Pennsylvania , Rhode Island , Vermont and Washington and the District of Columbia .
Soil @-@ Ozone occurs when pollutants from cars , power plants , oil refineries , chemical plants and other sources in the atmosphere react to sunlight .
The resulting smog can cause serious respiratory problems for sensitive groups of people , and contribute to thousands of premature deaths every year .
Representatives of the public health and environmental protection sector praised Pruitts for the surprising change of course .
&quot; It is disturbing how much pressure it takes to get this reasonable step from the person responsible for the protection of the air we breathe , &quot; said Lori Ann Burd of the Center for Biological Diversity .
&quot; We must continue to fight the ideological crusade of the Obama administration to soften the pressure on the environment and their special interests . &quot;
Tumlingen celebrates 750 years of St. Hilarius Church
750 Years of St. Hilarius Church : On the occasion of the anniversary of the mountain church , the old cemetery area in Tumlingen is invited to a servade on Sunday , July 16th at 4.30 p.m .
Whether its idyllic location and beautiful views of the village are concerned , the historical St. Hilarius complex is a popular place to stay .
Visitors to the sixth Benefiz @-@ Serenade can be enchanted by this powerful place .
The rescue and preservation of the oldest building in the village has made the Friends and Patrons of Tumlingens a matter of their concern .
Walter Martini emphasizes : &quot; It is a unique village gem . &quot;
In 1267 , the St. Hilariuskapelle was first mentioned in the document of the Württemberg : Count Hermann von Sulz sold the mountain with the parish church to Tumlingen and the Vogtei to Magister Eberhard von Horb and to his brother Dietrich for 20 Mark Silver .
According to the document , in addition to the former parish church , the Tumlinger Berg also had a magnificent courtyard , to which the church or the right to Patron belonged , and not the other way round .
So people lived there , too .
As the description of the Upper Freudenstadt from 1858 shows , the third , the village &apos;s dominion was , besides the lower village and the upper village , which is still visible in the village image , the &quot; mountain &quot; was the third , the ruler of the village .
Old maps from the year 1600 prove this historical aspect .
The right to Patron was created from the early medieval law of the church of Odra .
A master who built , equipped and occupied a church with a priest , remained the owner of the church and could inherit this church .
This was accompanied by the right to appoint a priest or to propose to the bishop a new pastor .
There were three altars in the church and two bells on the tower .
The larger of the two most crumble on Christmas 1673 .
The newly purchased bell was first sounded in 1692 , but had to be handed out later in World War II in 1942 .
During the Thirty Years War ( 1616 to 1648 ) , the window panes were knocked in and the bell parts were stolen .
The roof was leaking .
In 1796 , French troops were stationed in the vicinity of the church .
They broke the chests and church chairs out and used them as firewood .
The remains of the church threatened to fall in 1809 .
Until 1869 , the Tumlinger Hilarius Cemetery served as the last resting place for the dead from Hörschweiler .
In 1823 the cemetery was enlarged and the Hilariuskirche was abandoned .
Only the church tower was left standing .
On the New Year &apos;s Eve in 1834 , a storm broke out the church tower and half fell .
To prevent an accident , the tower was abandoned .
Only the tower dwarf , which is still present today , was preserved as a witness of the 750 @-@ year @-@ old church history .
Today , Horst Kallenberger and Rolf @-@ Dieter Wasem from the &quot; Schaffigen Rentdern &quot; ( Retire of the Schaffigen Rentals ) , as the successor of Dieter Dockhorn , are taking care of the Hilarius complex .
As the organizer , the friends and sponsors of Tumlingens on Sunday , July 16 , invite to the sixth Serenade at the old cemetery for the St. Hilariuskapelle .
This is being done by the Combo of the Kepler @-@ Gymnasium Freudenstadt and with papers by Kurt Kübler and Reinhold Matteis .
Local @-@ level local Hartmut Romann speaks the final word .
The program will start at 4.30 pm .
The entrance is from 3.30 pm .
At the end of the Serenade , a drink takes place in front of the cemetery .
&quot; Enjoy a summer evening under the shade of old trees and with a view over meadows to the forest , &quot; the organizers point out the fantastic view .
The servo takes place only in dry weather .
The organizers hope to have many supporters to preserve the historic St. Hilarius facility , which is of interest to tumble and auditory fans .
Friends and sponsors of Tumlingens are particularly committed to home care , as the Serenade organiser Walter Martini confirms .
Association wants to have windmills approval checked .
&quot; Boisheim fights &quot; does not see any legal requirement for the construction and has therefore turned to the Countryside .
The &quot; Boisheim Protested &quot; Association has asked Landrat Andreas Coenen ( CDU ) to withdraw his authorization to build planned wind turbines in the Boisheimer Nette .
It was unlawful .
The association argues that no wind turbines could have been approved at the time of the authorization on 30 December 2016 in the Boisheimer Nette , as at that time only a wind power concentration zone at the city limits to Schwalmtal had existed .
Within this concentration zone , the construction and operation of wind turbines are possible in principle , but at the same time the concentration zone eliminates the use of wind energy for the rest of the city .
Such zones are intended to prevent a &quot; spark &quot; of the landscape .
The city of Viersen had decided a second zone of wind energy concentration in the Boisheimer Nette by changing the land use plan a few days earlier - but this zone had not been approved by the Düsseldorf regional government due to a malform .
&quot; The lack of a planning requirement makes the issued permit decision unlawful and therefore also retracts from it , &quot; said Christoph Erkens , Chairman of the &quot; Boisheim Convention on the Protection of Data and Data of a Data Protection Act . &quot;
Current status of the situation : the town council decided once again on the wind concentration zone in the Boisheimer Nette before the summer break ; the regional government is pending .
Erkens : &quot; With this decision , the granting of an authorization for the installation of wind turbines is therefore excluded by planning law . &quot;
The circle confirms the receipt of the request .
&quot; The corresponding letters have been received by the Viersen District since last week , &quot; a speaker explained .
The circle will take the occasion to re @-@ examine the matter .
But they are not expected to stop the procedure .
In view of ongoing proceedings at the administrative court , the circle didn &apos;t want to express its content on the reasoning of the &quot; Boisheim Association . &quot;
NEW Re , a subsidiary of NEW , is planning to build four 200 meter high windmills on an area of the Boisheimer Nette .
Already last year , the company , in which the city of Viersen is indirectly involved , had signed a lease agreement with the owner of the approximately 65 hectares of land .
The four wind turbines are to be installed to meet the electricity needs of about 11,400 households - this is about one fifth of the electricity needs of all four @-@ tier households .
The NEW Re intends to invest about 20 million euros .
Last summer , Viersen &apos;s municipal council gave the go @-@ ahead for the purchase of three wind turbines in the Linnich wind farm near Aachen and a shareholding in a wind turbine plant in the district of Heinsberg in Übach @-@ Palenberg .
