Moores Ford massacre : activists are reviving racist lynchakt as a call for justice .
A 1948 @-@ he Ford Mercury drove through a group of viewers in the rural Monroe , Georgia , and stepped to the Ford bridge of Moore .
Before the car , which had four black passengers and a white driver , could reach the bridge , a group of white men with rifles appeared out to block its way .
The mob &apos;s leader - a man of middle age in a needle strip suit - took a long train on his cigar and looked through the windshield .
Inside , the occupants were in fear .
&quot; We want this n ---- r Roger ! &quot; the man was a breath of peace .
&quot; Get him out of the car &quot; !
Cry torn the silence when a young black man was pulled from the passenger seat .
Fortunately , this terrible scene , which was played on July 22 , is only a replica .
But when a blade is thrown around the neck of Roger , almost all of the people in the audience were suffering from very real sigh .
Since 2005 , civil rights activists have returned to Moore &apos;s Ford Bridge to imitate the night when two black couples - Roger and Dorothy Malcom as well as George and Mae Murray Dorsey - were lured in 1946 by Ku Klux Klan .
Nobody ever was accused of murder .
&quot; It is really incomprehensible that not a single person was arrested in all these years - although we see them in our communities , although we know who they are , &quot; said Tyrone Brooks , who is helping to organize the recreation .
&quot; It is a dirt spot on a story , but it is a burden for our souls . &quot;
Brooks is a 71 @-@ year @-@ old former member of the State of Georgia Congress and a civil rights activist for a lifetime .
For him , the question of enactment serves as a dramatic call for action and represents an annual reminder to the Monroe community that an injustice has never been corrected .
&quot; We want prosecution , we want to end , we want healing , we want reconciliation , but first we need to get justice , &quot; he said .
&quot; We cannot reach healing and reconciliation until we get justice . &quot;
A court doctor lays a blanket over the body of one of the victims of the Moore @-@ Ford @-@ Lynchen on 27 July 1946 .
According to FBI files received from the NBC News , more than 50 Monroe and surrounding counties were suspected in the Lynch process .
But nobody ever was accused .
Although the suspect has been at his own pace for decades , civil rights activists say that the double motivation for the Lynch actions has always been known throughout the city : politics and sex .
In April 1946 , a decision of the Supreme Court of Georgia enabled the black citizens in Georgia for the first time to cast their votes in the summer pre @-@ election campaign .
About at the same time as the election , according to the FBI , the black farmer Roger Malcom a white landowner , Barnett Hester , stalled during a battle - supposedly because of a woman .
Brooks said that the rumor in the city was that Hester had slept with Malcom &apos;s wife Dorothy and that the baby she was wearing was not from her husband .
On July 25 , 1946 , Loy Harrison , a prominent white landowner , paid 600 dollars to release Malcom from prison , the FBI said .
He was accompanied to the prison by Dorothy and her cousins Mae Murray and George Dorsey .
But when they moved away from the prison and approached the Moore &apos;s Ford Bridge , a Lynchmob surrounded the car .
&quot; It turns out that it was Harrison who prepared and ordered the murders , &quot; concludes a FBI report .
&quot; It is also obvious that there was a plot between the state and the local prosecution that not only involved in the murders , removed evidence and covered the identity of witnesses . &quot;
Brooks puts it even more concise .
&quot; It was a voting right massacre , &quot; he said .
&quot; They were all killed to send a message to black people in this community : &quot; If you register and if you vote , then this will happen to you here . &quot;
For almost a decade , Cassandra Greene has been managing the annual replica of the Moore Ford Bridge @-@ Lynch murders .
Their representation is descriptive , gripping and is founded in a kind of deep racism that many would like to believe no longer exists in America .
It is hard to watch , and even more difficult to look the other way , but Greene said that &apos;s the point .
&quot; We don &apos;t want to talk about the Lynch , because we will then be confronted with its ugliness in all of us , &quot; she said .
&quot; But I can &apos;t build a relationship with you , if you don &apos;t want to listen to me or don &apos;t want to hear what I &apos;ve gone through . &quot;
The rehearsals for this year &apos;s production started in June , and on the first day almost 30 actors - a dozen white and the rest black - rushed into a room and listened when Greene started a well @-@ balanced view of the show .
Wade Marbaugh is a Greenes Co @-@ Director .
He has been playing the role of a chief clan member for years , but he said that the role will never be easier .
&quot; I feel dirty because I &apos;m not that , &quot; said Marbaugh .
&quot; But I think it is very important to keep this story alive , because we don &apos;t want to return to these times . &quot;
On the other side of the room , the actors Darrius Bradshaw and Nick Rascona , who play Roger Malcom and Barnett Hester , tried their lines .
The song begins with Malcom , the hester cries and afflicts him , anger that he has been sleeping with his wife Dorothy .
&quot; I said n --- he , disappears of my property ! &quot;
Rascona redeems the fire .
There is a break .
And then , everyone ( at least all black actors ) breaks out in laughter .
Greene laughs the loudest .
&quot; What ? , &quot; asked Rascona .
He is alarmed and confused .
Was I too hard ?
&quot; No , &quot; Greene said , still kicking .
&quot; I just do not think that white people said &apos; n --- he &apos; at the time . &quot;
&quot; But , &quot; one of the other black actors twisted .
&quot; You just have to hit the hard &apos; R. &apos; .
And that &apos;s exactly where the energy in the room is shifting , a sense of comradeship replaces the discomfort .
Everyone is busy doing this , not only because of the game , but also because of the memory of the four people killed .
The scene begins again .
This time , when Rascona says the N word , he says it sharply and forcefully .
Absolutely nobody is laughing .
This repetition ends violently and quickly .
There are shouting , shooting and then silence .
A woman dressed with a mourning dress stands over the body of the actors and performs Billie Holidays with a mourning prayer &quot; Strange Fruit . &quot;
Your voice breaks with every second word .
About a hundred metres behind it , a group of about four dozen spectators wither and tears out of the eye .
The audience is divided almost equally between black and white .
A couple arrived from Ithaca , New York to see the show , many of them just come down the street .
They have all lived through Georgian moisture for almost eight hours , when Brooks takes them on a pilgrimage to the burial sites of the Malcoms and Dorseys .
The killings have remained an anecdote to this point , but now , when you are standing on the same land where the Lynch @-@ murders were committed , the air feels like a ghostly .
When the last notes of the song end , Greene invites the crowd to come closer and inspect the bodies .
Children hook on their heads , register the fake blood and the silent bodies and whisper each other .
&quot; They are not really dead , &quot; a little boy is murmeting as if he were thinking of remembering .
After a few minutes Greene thanks the crowd - and suddenly it is over .
The actors throw the Lynchseil away and wipe out their tears .
Bradshaw embraces Marbaugh , the man in the suit who played the clandestine member .
Nick Rascona embraces two of his fellow players and then crashes in their arms , and the emotional load of the performance finally breaks through with him .
&quot; It &apos;s okay , &quot; whispering one of the women .
&quot; We know that this is not in your heart .
You did this for one reason . &quot;
Later , the audience will meet the actors for dinner .
A white couple from Atlanta chats on a table , inspired by the creation of a black pair they just met .
Denise Duplinski fought to find words that left the feeling of the performance .
&quot; It is hard to hear these terrible words and deeds ... to come from people who look like you yourself and who do that because they look like you , &quot; she said .
&quot; What that really does with me makes me believe that the Lynch is still going on , &quot; she added , &quot; today it is only called a police shooting . &quot;
In the same room , Tyrone Brook &apos;s courtyard is at his table , he eats and reminds of the civil rights .
He said that he had already begun to plan the show next year .
&quot; The creation is , in a sense , inspiring , because it describes the horror of what we have endured , but it is also a moment of learning , &quot; he said .
&quot; It &apos;s so , if we don &apos;t learn anything from it , then it can happen again .
Those who do not study their own history , who do not know their own story , are doomed to repeat it . &quot;
