New results of the Swedish Assignment Age evaluation tests published .
The latest results of tests for asylum seekers in Sweden , which have been subjected to a new method of medical assessment of their age , show that more than 80 percent of the people tested are older than 18 years of age , but not everyone believes in the accuracy of these results .
The Swedish Forensic Medicine ( Rättsmedicinalverket ) has started to conduct the tests last year .
They are to make the assessment of the age more accurate during the asylum procedure , after the Swedish Immigration Office ( Migration Office ) was criticized for the errors in the assessment of the correct age of some refugees who were of the age of under @-@ age .
Until today , Migration Ket has sent out 6,880 instances to be tested and the Forensic Medicine Agency has now published the results of a total of 2,481 tests from the period from mid @-@ March to 31st July 2017 .
Eighty percent ( 2002 ) were rated as 18 or older , while in a further 25 cases the Forensic Medicine Agency assessed that the person was &quot; possibly 18 years or older . &quot;
In 432 cases , the conclusion was that the person is &quot; possibly under 18 years of age . &quot;
The tests are only performed in cases where the age of the person is questioned .
According to Johan Göransson from the Forensic Medicine Agency , the results have been consistent over the past months .
&quot; We do not see a big difference in statistics of the past months , the figures are quite constant , &quot; he said the magazine The Local .
The high percentage of cases in which the person was judged to be over 18 does not necessarily mean that the applicant has lied over his / her age .
In May , the agency published the results of 581 tests and said that there were 442 cases that &quot; propose that the person concerned be 18 years or older . &quot;
When the newspaper Svenska Dagbladet examined the 581 published results , it found out that in 243 of these cases the person had said that he would be 18 years old this year .
The method of medical age assessment , which makes X @-@ ray images of wisdom teeth and MRI images of cardiovascular joints , which are then analysed by dentists and radiologists , has also been criticized in some areas .
One skeptic is the endocrine inologist Claude Marcus of the Karolinska Institute , whose work includes the assessment of the biological maturity of people to see if they have passed through puberty sooner or later than usual .
&quot; I had reason to think about it and I do not think the type of measurement should be used .
Judging the biological age in this way is very uncertain .
If we can not say it for sure , we should not do it as if we could , &quot; he said to the newspaper Metro .
Göransson from the Forensic Medicine Agency , however , defended the accuracy of the tests : &quot; Generally , you can say that the risk of judging a child as an adult is low .
The greatest risk is that the person is just below 18 , in this case this is about 10 percent . &quot;
Full in the plan
