The Bank of England will keep interest rates at 0.25 % .
The Bank of England is expected to announce that interest rates will remain unchanged after the weak economic growth and the weakening of inflation have suppressed speculations of an increase .
The Bank of England has warned that economic growth will remain &quot; slow &quot; as it maintained family income interest rates in the midst of a growing pressure .
The Bank &apos;s Monetary Committee ( MPC ) policy makers voted with 6 to 2 to keep interest rates at 0.25 % , with less members aiming at an increase this month , as weak economic growth has weakened the support for an increase .
In its quarterly inflation report , the Bank reduced its growth forecasts to 1.7 % in 2017 and 1.6 % in 2018 , warning that the pressure on household income would continue , with inflation expected to rise to just under 3 % in the autumn .
But she signaled that in the years to come , interest rate increases would be necessary to curb the inflation fuelled by the country &apos;s accession and said that borrowing costs might have to rise more than expected in the financial markets .
Members also voted to withdraw a part of the Mammut Economic Package , which was released a year after the Treaty of Bretseover .
It will demand time for the plan to provide cheaper financial resources for banks from next February , although it has been said that it is now expected to provide 15 billion pounds more within the framework of this system - with 115 billion pounds of Sterling .
The Bank said just minutes after the decision on the interest rate : &quot; In the MPC &apos;s central forecast , the gross domestic product ( GDP ) remains weak in the short term , as the pressure on the real income of households continues to put on consumption . &quot;
At the interest rates , she repeated that &quot; a certain tightening of monetary policy &quot; was needed to cool down inflation and &quot; a bit larger &quot; than the markets expected .
Markets are presently forecasting the first quarter of the third quarter of next year and another in 2020 .
However , the Bank stressed that increases would be &quot; gradual &quot; and &quot; limited . &quot;
The Bank &apos;s downward @-@ adjusted growth forecasts for this and next year are in comparison to the forecast of 1.9 % and 1.7 % in May .
It has maintained its growth forecast to 1.8 % in 2018 .
The pound fell after these news against the dollar and the euro .
The pound fell by 0.5 % to 1.31 U.S. dollars and fell by 0.4 % to 1.11 euros .
The decision does not come about after the most recent disappointing growth figures have dampened the rising expectations of a rate increase and GDP in the second quarter has risen from 0.2 % in the previous three months to 0.3 % .
Growth is expected to be 0.3 % in the third quarter , although it will recover slightly after the bank at the end of the year .
The latest inflation report offered little enthusiasm for households affected by rising inflation and a slight increase in salaries , as the tightening will first get worse before it improves .
She added that monetary policy cannot &quot; prevent &quot; the loss of income in the next years , but she added that salaries will &quot; significantly &quot; recover at the end of their three @-@ year forecast .
The economy is also strengthened by the rising demand for British goods , which is due to the weak pound , which will compensate for a part of the lower consumption expenditure .
The decision comes a year after the lowering of interest rates to 0.25 % last August , after the shocking EU vote , which caused the pound to sink .
The Bank estimates that it will probably take four years to fully achieve this to the prices and the economy .
Before the Bank &apos;s last decision on interest rates , the call for a rise in interest rates had increased , as inflation increased the pressure on hard @-@ pressed households , with three policy @-@ makers demanding an increase to 0.5 % in July .
Last month , inflation fell from 2.9 % in June to 2.6 % , although the bank said that this was expected and that it would return to the bank in the months to come .
The Bank said that the excess compared to its 2 % target was &quot; entirely &quot; attributable to the weak pound .
Andrew Montlake , Director of Coreco Mortgage Brokers , said : &quot; Given the recent decline in the swap rates , the mortgage market should once again become more competitive , as the lenders are loudly searching for a business and want to end the year in a positive way .
The market looks particularly good for those who have to close the existing deals and the new mortgages , and many with toll @-@ free options and low rates are becoming more and more popular . &quot;
NHS ( National Health Service ) is to prescribe controversial HIV drugs to 10,000 people .
After a fight in the High Court against the funding of the drug in the NHS , patients will be given access to a &quot; transformative &quot; HIV prevention treatment from September .
NHS England announced that a pre @-@ exposation prophylaxis ( PrEP ) taken before sexual intercourse , which can reduce the risk of infection for high risk patients by about 86 percent , is now being delivered by the health service through a first three @-@ year study for an estimated 10,000 people .
He agreed to finance the process , after the appeal court confirmed a judgement of the Supreme Court last year that the NHS had the power to pay for the PrEP despite the assertion that the local authorities should finance it .
The move announced on Thursday was welcomed as &quot; an important new intervention &quot; by NHS England &apos;s Chief Executive Simon Stevens , while the National AIDS Trust ( NAT ) described this as a &quot; decisive moment . &quot;
Sexual health clinics in London , Brighton , Manchester , Liverpool and Sheffield will be among the first to allow people to apply , and other clinics will follow in October before it will be introduced in the UK in April 2018 .
Preexposation ( PrEP ) is a series of HIV drugs that are taken before sex in order to reduce the risk of HIV infection .
Studies have shown that PrEP significantly reduces the transmission of HIV in the case of infection .
Taking PrEP before sexual intercourse means that there are just enough drugs in a person &apos;s body to prevent an HIV infection .
Such drugs can be taken every day or so two hours before sexual intercourse .
Those who are eligible for treatment include gay or bisexual men , transsexuals and people with HIV positive partners who have not been treated successfully .
In hospitals , appropriate participants are identified who agree to the study , including men , women , transgenters and individuals with a partner whose HIV status is not known to be controlled by antiretroviral treatment .
People who live in England and are registered with a family doctor may also apply for a possible participation in their local participating sex clinic .
The program will cost £ 10 million and NHS England signed a contract to deliver medicines this week after an international competitive procurement .
The study is intended to assess the full potential of PrEP by collecting clinical evidence , as is specifically used , recorded and implemented on a large scale .
Stevens said , &quot; This major new intervention should supplement and overhaul the far @-@ reaching and increasingly successful HIV prevention efforts .
It is another milestone in the more than three decades of ongoing progress in the fight against one of the greatest health challenges in humanity . &quot;
Deborah Gold , Chief Executive at NAT ( National AIDS Trust ) , said : &quot; This is a crucial moment in the fight against HIV .
PrEP , if it is specifically aimed at people in need and at risk , offers the possibility to change the English HIV epidemic .
From September , people with a high risk of HIV will have access to this NHS @-@ funded attempt in England to develop a new instrument that is truly individually controlled and is not subject to negotiation with a partner , which will improve many , many lives .
We welcome this announcement . &quot;
Ian Green , Chairman of the Executive Board of the Terrence Higgins Trust , said : &quot; The priority is to ensure that the study is done quickly throughout the country and that nobody who is at risk of HIV is left behind .
Now that the PrEP test medicine has been obtained , we are on the way to protect more than 10,000 people infected with HIV . &quot;
Sharon Hodgson , the Shadow Health Minister , said : &quot; The beginning of the PrEP process is welcome and overdue after months of delays and hesitation in implementation by the government .
The evidence shows how transformative this drug can be as part of our HIV prevention approach and how to stop the transmission of this life @-@ changing infection .
This attempt takes us a step closer to understanding the advantages of PrEP .
Now it is important that this study is carried out as soon as possible throughout the country to protect people infected with HIV and that it will help us to make a step closer to ending the spread of HIV in society .
Living the big life : mezzanine and clever designer tricks have turned this small Earls Court apartment into a spacious home .
Joanne Leigh , a former banker with a passion for real estate , was reduced from a large house in Knightsbridge in 2014 to an Earls Court Apartment .
It is on the first floor of an elegant Queen Anne Terrace - and it is proof of the new design that Joanne didn &apos;t pack and sell her bags .
&quot; I had to live with my ex in Knightsbridge in a much larger room before , &quot; says Joanne .
&quot; I was looking for something that was comparable to my price .
I was shown this apartment in Earls Court and she had the same high ceilings .
I fell in love with her and made an offer on this day . &quot;
The apartment , which is 1000 square metres and is completely overtaken by the first visit .
Originally a one @-@ room @-@ house with a nested layout - you had to go through the kitchen to get to the bedroom - Joanne wanted to add a storage room and a mezzanine floor to make the most of the spacious ceiling .
&quot; I wanted high ceilings , large windows , a lot of light .
That was my requirement , &quot; she says .
To this end , she commissioned the architecture and design studio Duck &amp; Shed by Erfan Azadi of Notting Hill to use the 12.5 @-@ foot high ceilings and create a home that was not only a development but also a place to live for Joanne .
The ceiling was not large enough to place two rooms over each other , so the architectural challenge was to create an intermediate floor where you could stand .
Erfan had to think creatively to solve the challenge .
&quot; The steel mill is quite complicated , &quot; he explains .
&quot; We had to arrange the rooms in such a way that they were stacked on top of each other without having to make a bite .
It was not only about building a simple platform , but also about putting the steel in such a way that it had the right height for the function above or below . &quot;
This arrangement allowed Erfan to place two rooms in full height above the other - with a lot of additional drama .
A classic copper light window from Retrouvius overlooks the glamorous living area for the mezzanine floor .
Mirror @-@ lit gold furniture , silver accents and vintage Serge Mouille lighting set the style for the programme .
The use of mirrors plays a role in the addition of theatre , space and light .
On both ends a short corridor is mirrored to make it work longer .
In the kitchen the reflection is like a window in a different room .
At the entrance , mirrored units can accommodate all the cutlery and glass from Joanne .
By perceiving a more glamorous space , the beauty of the living room can block the kitchen if it is not used .
Catering and folding kitchens are nothing new .
The special thing about this is that it is a garage door .
&quot; A bit of engineering went in , &quot; explains Erfan from the steel @-@ reinforced , veneered MDF door .
&quot; It is counterbalanced , so that when you push the door , it slides very easily . &quot;
Thanks to the clever use of space and storage , Joanne has been able to win an additional 16 m ² , an additional bedroom and an improved layout .
&quot; At the end of the day you will sell the base surface - or how your property is valued , &quot; says Erfan .
&quot; A rudimentary form of a real estate agent will enter and perform a calculation based on the size of the land . &quot;
Here they have managed to increase the surface area and at the same time use high @-@ quality materials that give the room its luxurious advantage .
&quot; The materials are the things that affect you and that therefore help create an emotional bond in a home , &quot; says Erfan .
&quot; You don &apos;t fall in love with the electrical sockets in the walls .
These are important , but they do not play with your heart . &quot;
It shows that an apartment can turn into something amazing through good design and an increased square meter of something standard .
Indeed , like Joanne , you could decide not to leave this .
Brothers fight for a US $ 500 million property empire .
Two brothers , four decades after their Italian family founded the dynasty , are in a conflict about one of the largest real estate imperials in Victorias worth more than 500 million dollars .
The former director of the &apos; Carlton Football Club &apos; and developer Colin De Lutis lives in a villa in Toorak , only three doors away from his younger brother Paul , who has taken him to the Supreme Court to determine how their property and their business assets are to be divided .
Paul wanted to leave the family business as early as 2014 , but a sharp discussion about the separation is taking place at the Supreme Court .
Together with their mother Anna , the brothers own 38 properties worth more than half a billion dollars , accumulated since the 1973 family purchased a store on Smith Street , Collingwood .
The Westco Jeans family was founded there and grew into a chain of 160 retail shops .
It was sold in 1999 for 85 million dollars .
This money was invested in real estate and the portfolio of De Lutis now includes offices in the city centre , the development areas of Brunswick and Sorrento , the apartments in Gold Coast and Mt Buller , and shopping centres in the suburbs .
According to an agreement with her deceased father , Colin owns about two thirds of the company &apos;s assets and Paul about a third , described by Paul as &quot; the main operator and the dominant personality &quot; in the De Lutis family .
A court case states that this means that Colin owns 316 million dollars of property and Paul worth 176 million dollars .
The distribution of real estate and cash has proved to be a problem for Paul .
&quot; The division proposed by Colin left him with what I thought was the best objects , &quot; he wrote in 2015 in a first Affidavit .
Last Friday , the Supreme Court held its first hearing after several failed conciliations .
The brothers are now also fighting for control over the existing business - of which , as Paul claims , Colin has ruled out him .
Colin argues that this is not true , but accuses Paul of managing some possessions incompetently .
In an early Affidavit , which was submitted by Colin , he says that he hopes to settle the dispute .
&quot; My Italian heritage dictates that the family is the most important priority , &quot; he wrote .
The costs increase in the case , with hundreds of pages of Affidavits , emails and reports from companies , including Deloitte , Pitcher Partners and Charter Keck Cramer , and with leading lawyers , including Allan Myers , QC , and law firms held by both sides .
Mrs. De Lutis and her late husband Luigi were Italian immigrants who operated dairy bars and delicatessen shops from the late 1950 &quot; s and who bought a shop in Brunswick in the early 1970 &quot; s .
They used the security to buy the Collingwood Estate , where Colin opened the first jeans shop of the family .
Colin says that he began to work in Westco there when he was &quot; in my last years of high school , when Paul was still a little child ( about 10 years old ) . &quot;
Anna De Lutis says that she and Luigi , who died in 2012 , hoped to pass on their property to their sons in a amicable way .
&quot; Luigi often said to me that he never wanted the brothers to land in court , &quot; she wrote .
Two other brothers , Mark and Luke , are no longer part of the De Lutis group , Colin claiming that when leaving the company , Mark was paid 10 million dollars and Luke 26 million dollars .
Both Luke and Mark , he says , &quot; were bitter about the conditions of their financial separation . &quot;
Colin has been very reluctant , although he was a former member of the AFL Board of Directors and , after a biography from 2015 by Eddie McGuire , the best friend of the TV star .
Fairfax Media contacted Colin and Paul De Lutis and their attorneys .
Only Colin answered by SMS to say &quot; I can &apos;t really say much , &quot; because the case is before the courts .
&quot; It is very , very sad that my brother Paul decided to sue me and to take our surnames through the courts , &quot; he wrote .
In mid @-@ 2016 , Colin wrote to the court that he had attempted to meet the demands of his brother .
&quot; Our father would not have wanted Paul and I to end in our current dispute , &quot; he said and added that he &quot; still hoped for a solution . &quot;
On Friday , this hope seemed to have passed .
&quot; Our matter will be resolved by the courts in the near future , &quot; Colin wrote .
The new Chief of Staff of the Chief of Staff of the President is planning to limit the media abstention of the President .
Others have tried it and failed .
Maybe John F. Kelly can do it .
If so , he will be the first .
Politico reports that the White House &apos;s new Chief of Staff intends to limit the flow of information to President Obama - including the reports of the news media - hoping to keep the chief on a more even course .
Here is a piece by reporter Josh Dawsey :
&quot; When John F. Kelly , the new White House Chief of Staff , met with senior officials on his first day of work , he presented a major problem in President Donald Obama &apos;s White House that he wanted to fix : bad information that was in the hands of the President .
Kelly told the employees that information about him should flow - whether on paper or in briefings - because the President would make better decisions if he was given good information . &quot;
Kellys diagnosis makes sense , but others have tried to tame the country by monitoring its media budget .
President Obama &apos;s relationship to television goes back decades - and now that he is in the White House , his TV advertising is still strong .
&quot; If the candidate was upset about an unfair reporting , it was productive to show him that he received a fair reporting from the jobs that were to be convinced , &quot; said former election advisor Sam Nunberg in February to Politico .
Politicos Tara Palmeri wrote at the time that &quot; the key to control the Trumpet Twitter habit is to ensure , according to six former election officials , that his personal media consumption includes a constant praise . &quot;
Okay .
But the idea that the truck &apos;s Twitter habit has ever been &quot; under control &quot; is ridiculous .
Perhaps these campaign officials know something we don &apos;t know - that if they didn &apos;t do their jobs , they would have included more fuel .
We will probably never know anything about the tweets that you didn &apos;t send to us by the band .
If his colleagues have managed to keep him away from the hustle and bustle several times , then their efforts have been rewarded .
But nobody could have prevented the fact that the country was causing controversy .
Part of the problem is that people in a White House , made up of competing parties , are trying to continue their own agenda by presenting to the community material that may promote their views from the world .
