Antarctica : Giant mountain of ice in Antarctica .
In the western Antarctica , a gigantic iceberg has dissipated itself from the Schelfeis , which is almost seven times the size of Berlin .
The Bremerhavener Alfred Wegener Institute for Polar and Marine Research ( BWSE ) announced this on Wednesday .
The colossus , which weighs more than a trillion tons and is 5800 square kilometres , is now driving north and will probably take two or three years to melted .
It is one of the largest ice giants that researchers have registered in the last three decades , having a length of 175 km and a width of up to 50 km .
He does not pose a risk to people .
The extent to which climate change has played a role in the demolition of the iceberg is , according to the research data , unclear .
However , for about 30 years , there has been a series of unusual ice @-@ skating breakdowns in the region , writes the US Snow and Ice Research Center ( NSIDC ) in a fact background .
According to many researchers , this can be due to global warming .
The now abandoned colossus was so far part of the Larsen C bowl .
Schelfeis swims on the sea and is fed by large glaciers .
It is growing more and more into the sea and is disappearing regularly .
The Larsen C Schelfeis is located to the south of South America , almost at the top of the Antarctic Peninsula .
North of this skeletal ice , the balance of growth and disruption a few years ago was disturbed .
A complete temple feast was broken down there , writes AWI researcher Daniela Jansen .
There is a good deal to say that the Larsen C @-@ Schelfeis is in danger as well , as the front edge has never been as far away as it is at the moment .
Furthermore , the ice sheet is under tension and can fall apart if a piece of ice breaks out at a key point .
Melting ice and ice mountain does not increase the sea level , as they swim on the water and ice is supplanting as much water as they make after melting .
However , the loss of the ice of the shelter causes the glaciers to flow directly into the sea and to flow much faster than before .
This could possibly increase the sea level , but only to a small extent , writes the British research project on the observation of Antarctica ( Midas ) .
The Larsen C bowl is the fourth largest bowl in Antarctica , according to AWI specifications , with almost 50 000 square kilometres .
It is 700 meters thick on the land &apos;s transition and 200 meters on the front edge to the free ocean .
It breaks down there regularly .
&quot; Although it is a natural event and we are not aware of any direct climate change link , the ice sheet is now in a more dangerous position , &quot; said Martin O &apos;Leary of Swansea University , UK .
&quot; This is the widest retreat since the beginning of the records , &quot; he also confirmed .
Among scientists , it is controversial whether the Larsen C @-@ Schelfeis will now grow again or will go back in the long run and possibly collapse , said his colleague Adrian Luckman and added : &quot; Our models say that it will be less stable . &quot;
But this development will take decades .
Although these researchers do not trace the collapse of this gigantic iceberg to climate change , in the past decades a great many ice cubes of the Antarctic Peninsula have collapsed .
This long tip of the Antarctic extends to South America .
According to the British Antarctic Survey research programme , the Antarctic Peninsula &apos;s ice surface has fallen by 28 000 square kilometres since the 1950s .
The rise in temperature had contributed to the decline .
The Antarctic Peninsula has warmed up by about three degrees in the last 50 years and is thus one of the areas where the temperature is rising the fastest .
