The murder of a journalist underlines the growing threat in Mexico .
The staff of the weekly Riodoce meet normally on Wednesday to review their plans for the recent chaos in the state of Sinaloa by organized crime , corrupt officials and unremitting drug wars .
But on that day , in the shadow of their own tragedy , they came together to talk about security .
They were told that it is important to change their routines .
Two high @-@ ranking journalists discuss what is more secure : they should take their children to the office , who in 2009 were the target of an assault with grenades or leave them at home .
Security experts have written three words on a table on the front of the room : opponents , neutrals , allies .
You ask the reporters to propose names for each column - no proof is necessary , perceptions and belly feelings are sufficient .
There are drug smugglers , politicians , businessmen , journalists suspected of being on the government &apos;s or the cartel &apos;s payroll , a catalogue of criminals that make the chaos of Mexico dangerous .
There is no rest from the violence and while the corpses are accumulating throughout the country , more and more journalists are present : at least 25 since the beginning of the reign of President Enrique Pena Nieto in December 2012 , 589 are under attack and threatened by the Federal Protection and so far at least seven deaths have taken place in seven countries .
Among the latter , is her editor and her inspiration from Riodoce , the co @-@ founder of Javier Valdez Cardenas .
&quot; The greatest mistake is to live in Mexico and to be a journalist , &quot; wrote Valdez in one of his many books on violence related to drugs .
Its absence is felt deeply , although its presence is everywhere - a large photo of Valdez showing his middle finger with the word &quot; Justice &quot; is hanging on the facade of the Riodoce building . Two reporters in the 1930 &apos;s , Aaron Ibarra and Miriam Ramirez , wear t @-@ shirts showing his smiling face with glasses or his typical Panama hat .
The workshop takes place less than two months after his death , and the reporters discuss their shared topics , the trauma , their nightmares , insomnia , and paranoia .
Although a special Federal Attorney &apos;s Office was set up in 2010 to deal with the cases of journalists , the Federal Attorney General , according to the Committee on the Protection of Journalists , has only initiated two criminal proceedings .
As with most of the thousands of murders that are linked to drug trade and organized crime each year , journalists rarely try the murderers .
At Riodoce , they are still insisting on treating the violence of Sinaloa , although they are uncomforting and , although the terrain is more treacherous .
Without information about the murderers , without justice , the meeting to discuss the security has little use , says Ibarra .
&quot; It &apos;s very stupid to waste my time in this workshop , &quot; he says .
&quot; As long as we do not know the why , we all distrust . &quot;
On the morning of May 15th , Valdez left the office of the newspaper Riodoce in the capital of the province of Culiacan .
He only managed to drive a few blocks far before his red toyota Corolla was stopped by two men , being forced out of his car and shot on him 12 times , presumably because of the newspaper &apos;s name , which is translated as &quot; Twelfth River . &quot;
The shooter drove away in his car and damaged it in the vicinity .
Valdez was 50 years old .
He left a woman and two adult children .
Rumors tend to fly freely around Culiacan .
But there is practically nothing about the subject of Valdez than silence .
Since Guzman &apos;s arrest last year and his delivery to the United States in January , Sinaloa had become one of the most bloody battlefields in the country , as rival factions fight here to fill the vacuum .
It doesn &apos;t matter that in Sinaloa every day someone or more people are shot on the street or that the cemetery is full of decorated , two @-@ storey mausoleums for drug kings , which are larger than many houses for the living .
Forget for a minute that there is a state of &quot; peace &quot; if only one cartel has control over the coastal state with its valuable ports and drug trade routes to the United States .
Nevertheless , and despite the fact that Valdez was aware of the dangers of his work , Ismail Bojorquez ( 60 ) , a co @-@ founder and director of Riodoce , was broken with the guilt that he could not protect his friend .
He believes that two misunderstandings have contributed to the murder .
At first , an interview with Damaso Lopez , a leader of one of the competing cartel fractions in the war with Guzman &apos;s sons , was published in February .
This release may upset the sons , but suspected gang members bought each copy of the issue as soon as it was delivered to newspaper kiosks .
The second mistake was not to force Valdez to leave the country for his own safety after the confiscation of another newspaper that had published the same story .
Valdez was a legend in Mexico and abroad , and his killing is considered a milestone in Mexican violence against journalists .
He had survived for so long that his friends and colleagues assumed that he would always be there .
He was a reporter to Noroeste in 2003 , joining forces with five colleagues to found Riodoce and selling US $ 50 in equity .
In Sinaloa , &quot; it was impossible to pursue journalism without encountering the drug problem , &quot; said Bojorquez .
Over time , the newspaper has earned a reputation for courageous and honest reporting , and sales and advertising have increased .
Reporters loved to be able to publish hard @-@ hitting research without fear of censorship , and readers were fascinated by a publication in which they could read stories that nobody else dared to deal with .
Eight years after the foundation of Riodoce , she was awarded the prestigious &quot; Maria Moors Cabot Prize &quot; for her work in Latin America .
In the same year , Valdez won the International Press Freedom Award of the Committee for journalists &apos; Protection for his courage in the pursuit of the Mexican drug history wherever she was .
He voluntarily admitted that he was afraid .
&quot; I want to live on , &quot; he said at the time of the CPJ award .
Drug trade in Sinaloa &quot; is a way of life , &quot; he said last October in an interview with Rompeviento TV .
&quot; You have to take on the task that is your right as a journalist - either that or you play the stupid .
I don &apos;t want to be asked : &quot; What have you done with so many dead ... why didn &apos;t you say what was going on ? &quot;
The Riodoce team is missing Valdez , the fun bird who could go as a dockworker , the friend who was generous with hugs and advice , a teacher who knew how to survive .
They trusted in his routine .
He would always wear his hat .
He would always go to El Guayabo , the bar in front of the office , and always sit at the same table .
Now they ask : Was his love for routine his destruction ?
His death has forced them to question their own assumptions about how to do their work and how to stay alive .
&quot; You don &apos;t like it if you are concerned with their women , their children , their clean shops , their secret runways &quot; to move drugs .
&quot; These things were not allowed , &quot; said Bojorquez .
The result is , even in the best times , a high degree of self @-@ censorship and self @-@ preservation .
Trust your own instincts .
If it smells wrong , then it stays away from it .
The anger is , the editor of the Riodoce Andres Villarreal said that &quot; smell a sense that can be deceived ... and then the matter has happened to Javier . &quot;
The old rules , as he and others say , no longer apply in Sinaloa - just as they do in Tamaulipas , Veracruz , Guerrero and other states , where a toxic mixture of lucrative smuggling routes , weak institutions and corrupt government officials is at home .
The landscape is constantly changing .
In the space where the Riodoce employees met for security training , suddenly no mobile phones were allowed , and days earlier it was announced that spyware , which is sold exclusively to governments , was used to monitor journalists and activists in Mexico .
Outside , two policemen sought refuge from the heat of 104 degrees ( 40 ° C ) in the shade of a tree .
They were assigned by the provincial government to protect the Riodoce offices in a four @-@ storey building in a middle class quarter of Culiacan .
Half joking , some of the reporters wondered if these officers are among the 50 percent of the policemen the Governor himself described as untrustworthy .
It has been months since the reporters went to the mountain landscape , where the drug @-@ related groups are in fact under control .
For this week &apos;s edition , Riodoce has focused on three main stories .
There was the murder of the brother of the former well @-@ known boxer Julio Cesar Chavez in Sinaloa .
They also had an exposition on the public spending , which concentrated on the governor &apos;s hometown .
And there was a group that carried out a kidnapping in one of the most expensive restaurants in Culiacan , a block from the Prosecutor &apos;s Office .
There was no official word about who was kidnapped or how it was done , so be careful when it was time to write what everyone in the city knows : that the restaurant is a favorite place of drug dealers and authorities .
A reporter learned from public records that the restaurant was registered under the name of a politician , a member of the ruling Institutional Revolutionary Party ( PRI ) , which ruled almost the whole century .
Several PRI governors have recently been accused of high @-@ profile corruption cases .
Villarreal asked the reporter to write about the events that had happened in this place , including one in which a son of &quot; El Chapo &quot; Guzman escaped a military raid .
Just a few months earlier , readers would have considered the Valdez columns to be the best source of information about the kidnapping .
&quot; We would have known what happened in the past , &quot; said Villarreal , 46 , with the nickname &quot; El Flaco &quot; for its slim body shape .
&quot; Now all channels of communication with our sources have been broken . &quot;
The Valdez office was turned into a storage room for signs and labels protesting against the murders of journalists , because the employees became what they would never have expected themselves : activists on behalf of the press .
Reporter Miriam Ramirez seized a few characters and the next morning went to a demonstration for another journalist to the office of the prosecutor .
Salvador Adame disappeared three days after Valdez was killed in the western state of Michoacan .
A burned body was discovered and officials say , based on DNA tests that it is his body .
Journalists throughout the country have become louder by writing &quot; SOS Press &quot; in organized protests on pavements and buildings .
On this day , the director Bojorquez is away in Washington and is trying to gather international support for the judiciary in the Valdez case .
In a meeting with the Governor of Sinaloa the day after the murder of Valdez , Ramirez accused the authorities of spying out journalists and letting them kill them because they told the truth .
She has asked for it to be reorganized by the government since she is concerned that her anger has violated her objectivity .
Valdez had repeatedly said that journalists in Mexico were &quot; surrounded &quot; by organized crimes , government officials involved in them and an indifferent society .
In his last book &quot; Narco @-@ Journalism , &quot; he wrote that reporters are not only killed by drug groups , but also on the command of politicians and security forces that were in the same blanket with organized crime .
The media guard group &quot; Articulo 19 &quot; has allocated more than half of the attacks on journalists to the police and officials in the past year .
&quot; In Mexico you die because they want you to keep your mouth shut , &quot; said Ramirez .
Obviously , the murders have a dissuasive effect .
Nobody is forgetting the death of the blogger Maria Elizabeth Macias in the northern border state of Tamaulipas six years ago .
Her body was found along with a note that was supposedly signed by the zaar cartel : &quot; Here I am because of my reports . &quot;
A computer keypad and headphones were located next to their separated head .
Some branches have chosen to close , such as the newspaper El Norte in the northern border state of Chihuahua after the murder of the correspondent Miroslava Breach in March .
Others continue to do so , as El Manana of Nuevo Laredo did after the murder of its director in 2004 .
In 2010 , the Diario de Ciudad Juarez was directed to the drug cartels with a leading article on the title page with the title &quot; What do you want ? &quot;
Some journalists have fled from their homelands or even from the country .
It is a painful decision .
It is hard to find work in exile , and they are still watching the streets and looking for dangers .
And sometimes they are hunted , as the photographer Ruben Espinosa , who was murdered in 2015 in an apartment in Mexico City three months after the flight from Veracruz , with four women .
For those who are left behind and continue the work , it is a daily dance with risky decisions .
Ibarra , who wanted to be a poet , admits that the drug trade is frightening him .
&quot; Mexico goes to the hell , and that &apos;s why I became a reporter , &quot; he said .
At midnight , the last Friday , when the last edition was already finished , the editors of Riodoce sat on the sidewalk in front of the office and drank beer , when at once all their phones began to sum up .
A series of shootings with bandits and security forces near the seaside village of Mazatlan had left 19 confirmed dead .
The war escalated further , as promised by a series of cartel messages in the region .
From the edge of the brick , they have published the news on the Riodoce website about mobile phones .
The title page had to be changed the next day .
Sirens were in the vicinity of the castle , another shoot @-@ out in the area .
Bojorquez took a look at the policemen who held guards to see if they were on standby .
If they were afraid , they didn &apos;t show it .
The riodoce staff followed his footsteps under the mighty portrait of the fallen founder of the newspaper , whose middle finger was visible for the whole of Sinaloa .
&quot; How can you even think of closing , &quot; Bojorquez said , &quot; if the same day that Javier was murdered , the trainee asked me to send her to the streets to report . &quot;
Dinosaurs used camouflage to avoid their opponents .
At first glance , it looks like a statue , albeit a terrifying one .
Now researchers have found out that the first findings of the incredible rhinocery of a Canadian miner have been obtained so well that experts have described it as &quot; Mona Lisa of dinosaurs . &quot;
It was so well preserved that they even realized that his scaly skin was of red brown - and they said they had something of a &quot; troubled past . &quot;
The report in the magazine &quot; Current Biology &quot; described him as &quot; the best preserved armored dinosaurs ever found and one of the best dinosaurs in the world . &quot;
The fossil is a newly discovered type of nodosaurian , which lived in the middle of the creid period between 110 million and 112 million years ago .
Its incredibly well @-@ preserved condition has astonished researchers who describe it as &quot; truly remarkable . &quot;
According to reports in National Geographic , the armored herbivored is the best preserved fossil of its type .
It was found by Shawn Funk when he excavated on 21 March 2011 in the Suncor Millenium Mine near Fort McMurray in the North of Alberta , Canada .
He met something that seemed to be out of place from the surrounding rock and decided to look more closely .
The fossil he discovered was sent to the Royal Tyrrell Museum of Palaeontology .
They spent the next six years to discover the wild animal within the 2,500 pound ( 1,100 kg ) heavy earth clumping .
After all this hard work , the final result is ready to be unveiled .
&quot; If you just blinking a bit with your eyes , you might almost believe that it has been sleeping , &quot; said the main author Caleb Brown , scientist at the Royal Tyrrell Museum , where the creature is to be seen .
&quot; It will be one of the most beautiful and best preserved dinosaurs in scientific history - the Mona Lisa of the dinosaurs . &quot;
Researchers have found that , although this herbivodist is covered with an armour and resembles a changing tank , it was threatened by a carnivorous dinosaurs .
The strongly armored dinosaurs used a red and white camouflage to hide from predators and used a shield technique known as countershading , which is also used by many modern animals .
This would have allowed one to get better in the surroundings and to avoid one &apos;s opponents , say experts , suggesting that it was threatened by larger dinosaurs .
The 110 million @-@ year @-@ old creature from the Nodosaurian family made the first headlines in May and is now on display at the Royal Tyrrell Museum of Palaeontology .
Researchers have studied the wild animal and named it Borealopelta markmitchelli after the museum technician Mark Mitchell , one of a team that spent more than 7,000 hours revealing it .
The amazing preservation of the specimen allowed Dr. Mitchell and an international team of scientists to document the pattern and shape of drawers and armors over his body .
They discovered the countershading , a common defence method in the animal kingdom , which means that the top of the creature was darker than its base .
Although the countershading is common , the results are surprising , as the size of Borealopelta is far greater than that of the animals living today .
It suggests that the dinosaurs were driven by enough pressure from carnivorous dinosaurs , that evolution favored the disguise of confrontation as a medium of survival .
But most contemporary animals that use the countershading - think of deer , zebras or belts - are much smaller and more vulnerable than prey , which indicates that this nodosaurian was exposed to a real fight for survival .
&quot; Strong predatory behaviour on a massive , heavily armored dinosaurs shows how dangerous the dinosaurs predators must have been in the creation period , &quot; Brown said .
The chemical analysis of organic compounds in their scales also shows that the dinosaurs &quot; skin was red brown .
Scientists are also examining the animal for evidence of its life , including its preserved intestine , to figure out what it eaten as its last meal .
They believe that the dinosaurs , when they died , were thrown into a river and driven out to the sea , where they sank on the back to the sea bed .
At that time , Alberta was as warm as Southern Florida today , and rivers and oceans probably spread far further inland than they are today .
&quot; This nodosaurian is truly remarkable , because it is completely covered with preserved scaly skin , but also remains in three dimensions and maintains the original shape of the animal , &quot; said Brown .
&quot; The result is that the animal looks almost the same today as it did in the early creation period .
You don &apos;t need to use a lot of imagination to reconstruct it , and if you just have a little bit of your eyes , you might almost believe that he is sleeping . &quot;
The creation period was a time when giant theropods , carnivorous dinosaurs standing on two legs , crossed the earth .
Although the King of them , the Tyrannosaurus Rex , lived millions of years to Borealopelta , the armored dinosaurs were hunted by some of his frightening ancestors .
This includes the acrocanthosaurus , a 38 foot ( 11.5 m ) long , six @-@ ton monster .
The scientists , whose latest findings are published in the current Biology , believe that Borealopelta was sank in the sea after death and was mumbled in mud .
The creature was found by Shawn Funk on March 21 , 2011 when he excavated in the Suncor Millenium Mine near Fort McMurray in the North of Alberta , Canada .
According to the museum , it is the best preserved armored dinosaurs of the world , including skin and armour , and is complete from the muzzle to the hips .
The average creatures were about 18 feet ( 5 meters ) long and weighed up to 3,000 pounds ( 1,300 kg ) .
It included two 20 @-@ inch long tips that were weighed by his shoulders .
The researchers believe that the armored herbivored by western Canada was washed until a flooded river flooded it into the open sea .
But the underwater coating of the dinosaurs kept his armour in exquisite detail .
The fossilized remains of this special type are so well preserved that the skin remains still cover wooden panelled panels along the dinosaurs .
As Michael Greshko wrote for National Geographic , such a level of conservation is &apos; rare as a gain in the lotto .
The more I look at it , the more confusing it will be .
Stretched remains of the skin are still covered by wooden pane panels that wear the animal &apos;s skull .
His right @-@ hand front foot is on his side , his five fingers are spun upwards .
I can count the drawers on his soles &apos; .
In the storm : mass carambolage on A8 : many injured .
Hard accident on Thursday evening on the A8 : Almost 30 vehicles are involved in a mass carambolage .
