Deutsche Bahn : Group testing &quot; Comfort Check @-@ in &quot;
It is a moment that almost everyone has ever experienced : Nothing imagining you &apos;re sitting in the train and sleeping .
Or you are more deeply involved in your work and have a very good thoughts .
And then : pufff .
The creator comes and awakens an ungentle or tears the passenger out of the workflow .
Ticket control .
But the times of interruptions are soon over .
Deutsche Bahn is currently testing the control of the ticket without any effort .
You can simply confirm by mobile that you are taking your seat and devaluing the ticket .
Currently , a test operation is running on two routes .
&quot; If the offer is of course relevant , we will expand it .
It will be available on many other long @-@ distance routes in Germany at the beginning of next year , &quot; said a railway speaker .
&quot; Comfort Check @-@ in &quot; is the name of the train to devalue the ticket without the creator .
To use them , however , a number of requirements have to be fulfilled : You need a smartphone or a tablet .
And you have to have booked an online @-@ ticket - which is already the case for most passengers .
You also have to buy it via the railway app that DB Navigator purchased , which is the most convenient way to book a ticket .
And finally , a seat reservation is needed .
The comfort check @-@ in works like this : you get into the train , call your ticket under &quot; My Tickets . &quot;
A button for electronic devaluation will appear in the test customers .
The train attendants will receive a message on their mobile devices that Passenger X is in the train and has taken the booked seat Y .
If the train attendant comes to the ticket control room Y , he / she knows that there is a passenger with a devalued online ticket .
So you can sleep , continue to work , and let &apos;s not be too surprised .
The &quot; check @-@ in &quot; has two advantages : one for the customer , one for the railway - which should be used by the passengers .
&quot; If you want to travel freely without a ticket control through our train attendants , simply check in at the DB Navigator .
This allows passengers to make their travel time more personal in the train , &quot; says Michael Peterson , Marketing Board of DB Fernverkehr .
Second plus : &quot; And our employees have more time than they do today to meet the individual needs of our customers . &quot;
Those who travel by train often know that , besides delays and missed trains , the ticket controls are one of the greatest disadvantages .
Often , one has to hand , but in any case one of the other travelers does not have all the documents one needs to devalue online @-@ tickets .
You will need a credit card or a ID or any other document that you need to present .
The result is endless discussions between train and passenger , which leads to bad mood in the compartment or the big room and delays in the control of the ticket and takes the time to take care of other concerns .
Only a small number of test customers can use the new service .
And only on the connections ICE 915 Dortmund @-@ Stuttgart and ICE 912 Stuttgart @-@ Essen .
More train numbers on the same route are not yet available .
How an activist came to learn the answer to the question : &quot; Whose Streets ? . &quot;
On the 9th of August 2014 , Brittany Ferrell was shaken to the core .
Only one day after she arrived in New York City , the birdies in St. Louis sat on the bed of an Airbnb apartment , which she hired and scrolled through Facebook on her phone .
She stumbled about a post from someone from the High School .
&quot; The police has just killed an 18 @-@ year @-@ old child , and it is still on the streets , &quot; it was said .
Confused , as nobody else posted the information on her feed , she closed Facebook and opened Twitter .
A user with the Handle @ TheePharoah was constantly reweaded on her timeline .
The rapper from the area of St. Louis was about to live the death of Michael Brown , an unarmed black 18 @-@ year @-@ old killed by a white policeman in Ferguson , Mon .
He posted a picture of Brown &apos;s lifeless body , which was stretched out on the street and would stay there for four hours .
&quot; That &apos;s wild , &quot; thought Ferrell .
But death was nothing new for them and their community .
She dropped out of her cell phone and returned to the evening to get tweets about people on the Canfield drive .
There were photos of the police band and people who were screaming , and of a man claiming to be Brown &apos;s father , who had a sign saying , &quot; Ferguson &apos;s police just killed my unarmed son ! . &quot;
She observed a live feed , where a policeman stood before a group of demonstrators with a selling dog .
In another video , she said , another policeman disposed of his rifle and cried , &quot; I &apos;ll kill you ... animals all . &quot;
&quot; That &apos;s not usual , &quot; Ferrell reminded himself of her statement .
&quot; That &apos;s not the kind of murder we got used to and we can call it typical . &quot;
At this moment I felt chequered .
I was angry and cried . &quot;
She booked a return flight and went home the next day .
After the landing at 11 p.m. , she picked up her 6 @-@ year @-@ old daughter from the house of her father and decided that the next morning the two would go to Ferguson .
What happened to Ferrell and Ferguson in the following three years is the theme of a new documentary , &quot; Whose Streets ? , &quot; in the cinemas from the 11th of September .
It is a story of survival and protest , love and loss , strength and strength , and resistance , which consists of the point of view of people who live in the community and have filled the streets to ask for answers .
When Ferrell drove to Ferguson with a daughter , who wore a flower dress and a matching crown , they talked a conversation that they had a lot of times before this morning about the black experience and the black condition .
&quot; You remember how I taught you , when black people have to fight for what they believe ? &quot; She remembered what she said .
&quot; We are going to Ferguson now because the police killed an 18 @-@ year @-@ old boy and that was not right .
I could not take it with me , &quot; Ferrell said later .
&quot; What has happened today is a peak of what has happened in the past .
She has to know it and see it and be brought into it .
It must be well developed , not only activists and organization , but also know the different strata of the black experience and the black constitution and what we have to do to become free to liberate ourselves .
Although I am lucky to give her an experience where she personally does not experience any blatant racism , she is no more than the next black woman child .
I have the feeling that I would be doing her a disservice to keep her away from it . &quot;
The first station , when they arrived in Ferguson , was on South Florissant Road , a place opposite the police , which became a campsite , when activists were waiting for the possible non @-@ prosecution of Officer Darren Wilson .
There , Ferrell and his daughter connected themselves with a local business owner , who made lunch .
They prepared bags and helped to pass them on to demonstrators .
Slowly a new type of activism for Ferrell began to take shape .
&quot; My Activism changed completely after August 9 , &quot; she said and notes that as a student at the University of Missouri @-@ St. Louis she was president of the Minority Student Nurses Association and focused on food justice and health inequalities in black communities .
&quot; I had no experience in organizing .
Everything is done . &quot;
Weeks later , Ferrell met Sabaah Folayan , one of the film &apos;s co @-@ directors , in an evening demonstration .
Folayan said that she and her cameraman Lucas Alvarado @-@ Farrar only wanted to &quot; document &quot; what happened .
Ferrell asked a question : &quot; You want to document or you are trying to find a story that you can use ? &quot;
This skepticism was nourished by countless cases of people , often white people , in a community that benefited from their pain and resistance .
As Folayan recently explained in an interview : &quot; We had heard that people born and raised in Ferguson didn &apos;t centered their voices and we wanted to do it differently . &quot;
Folayan had already worked with the former co @-@ director Damon Davis , an artist from the field known for his active interest in the death row .
Ferrell brought the project closer , and the Whose Streets team , together with six other locals , followed in the years after Brown &apos;s shooting .
&quot; This documentary is not someone who speaks for us or speaks to us , &quot; said Davis .
&quot; That was the main thing for me , how these people are presented , because that is how I will be represented . &quot;
The aim was not to concentrate on Ferrell &apos;s experience - to show that the movement is not a Messiah leader , but a community that comes together , &quot; Folayan said - it proved to be the most open participant in the film .
As a result , documents by &quot; Whose Streets ? &quot; reveal surprisingly personal aspects of Ferrell &apos;s life , how she falls in love and how she married .
&quot; I felt naked , &quot; Ferrell said and laughed when she saw the finished product for the first time .
&quot; But I understood that there must be a certain degree of vulnerability in this work and in the humanization of black people . &quot;
In addition , Ferrell &apos;s story repeats the role of black gay women in the wider Black Lives Matter movement .
Two of the three founders of the movement , who marked the film &quot; BlacklivesMatter &quot; in social media after the killing of the black teenager trayvon Martin in 2012 , identify themselves as gay .
&quot; black gay women in leadership positions have carried the movement as a whole , &quot; Ferrell said , &quot; and that &apos;s why we are constantly fighting on different fronts .
Black gay women have to bear the main burden of everything . &quot;
Folayan agreed to note that &quot; the movement is really maintained by black women and many black women . &quot;
However , she insists that &quot; this is not an affirmative type of action , &quot; in which Ferrell was chosen as the heart of the film for her identity .
&quot; She was the person who sparked this energy , &quot; she said .
&quot; It is not a coincidence , although she was on the front line , because in a life at these crossings as a black gay woman , the stakes are so high .
You can feel their energy through the screen . &quot;
Meanwhile , the news cameras in Ferguson have long since disappeared , but the racial connections between the community and the police are still in place .
Ferrell is on a two and a half years &apos; interval due to the blockage of a motorway at a demonstration .
She describes the social and political atmosphere of the area , which she calls her home : &quot; A chaos . &quot;
Yet she clings to a song and feels committed to this memory since she took to the streets in Ferguson three years ago .
&quot; It is our duty to fight for our freedom .
It is our duty to win .
We must love and support each other .
We have nothing to lose - except our chains . &quot;
From &quot; Dream &quot; to &quot; Size Wrestling &quot; : TV readers discuss Bit Gallery .
