But for a farmer market hall there is no place anymore thanks to the insane many - as I can find - useless construction projects in Bitburg .
When the farmhouse is damaged , a piece of culture will disappear . &quot;
A matching : Artist creates portraits with Chelsea Mannings DNA .
In the middle of the space of the Fridman Gallery in New York are many faces - white , black and brown , almost imperceptibly the same - hanging on the ceiling cables .
The 30 portraits were created by the artist Heather Dewey @-@ Hagborg , with wall tubs and hair cuts sent to her by Chelsea Manning .
Manning sent the excerpts from Fort Leavenworth prison , where the former secret service analyst spent 35 years in prison after making public diplomatic reports classified as secret on the WikiLeaks website .
The new exhibition , which was opened on 2nd August , is called A Becoming Resemblance ( A Suitable Similarity ) .
She is the product of two years of correspondence between Dewey @-@ Hagborg , whose discipline united their expertise in technology , computer sciences and art , and Manning , a transwoman and a groundbreaking disciple whose convictions were turned from Barack Obama when he was only three days in office .
Through the algorithmic analysis of the DNA extracted by Manning , from which 30 portraits were created , how someone could look with these genomic data , Dewey @-@ Hagborg created a sharp , albeit intellectual comment not only on the malleability of DNA data - the many ways in which they can be interpreted and the inherent determinism of these interpretations - but also on identity .
Manning , born as Bradley Edward , but has already been talking openly about the identification of women since her puberty , was sentenced in 2013 for 20 charges , including six crimes against the law on spy , computer fraud and theft .
The jail suppressed her image , so much so that there was only one photo - a grained black and white selfishness , in which Manning is visibly uncomfortable sitting on a driver &apos;s seat and wearing a platinblonde wig - to which she was associated .
In 2015 , Dewey @-@ Hagborg was just the winner of the great success of her 2012 project &apos; Stranger Visions &apos; .
In the exhibition , the artist produced portraits of strangers from forensic artefacts such as cigarette mute and chewing gum , extracting the DNA from the waste to create an image of what these people could look like .
That was the time she received an email from a newspaper magazine .
&quot; They conducted an interview with Chelsea Manning while in prison and they wanted a kind of portrait to accompany this article , &quot; Dewey @-@ Hagborg explained at a press preview of the new exhibition .
And she could not visit and could not be photographed at this time , so she contacted Chelsea asking if she wanted to make a DNA portrait .
The artist and her imprisoned muse became unequal pamphlets , who exchanged several letters in the course of two years .
They even created a comic &quot; Suppressed Images , &quot; illustrated by Shoili Kanungo , which provided a future in which the President of Chelsea would convert the judgement and would be able to see the exhibition in person .
That Obama would actually demand the release of the book only a few days after the book was published , after seven years of brutality in a military prison in Kansas , was a welcome kind of happy meeting .
There is also something deep and strong at the opening of the exhibition , as Donald Sturm is waging a war against informants from the government camp and against Transgender troops to find new , tweeting @-@ capable ways to continue to expose the rigid divisions that he should actually resolve .
When asked about the strange timing of all these things , Dewey @-@ Hagborg said that &quot; things happen for a certain reason . &quot;
In an artistic statement written on the wall of the gallery , Manning , with its oversized signature ( the &quot; i &quot; in its surname is endowed with a heart ) , called for an end to the &quot; automatic splitting , which was the basis of sex , race , sexuality and culture . &quot;
On the opposite wall , its mitochondrial DNA sequence - a thousand feet of Cs and Gs and As , is written in pencil .
Dewey @-@ Hagborg hoped , by incorporating the 200 letters ( or nucleotides according to the artist &apos;s sophisticated scientific lexicon ) , to show how surprisingly similar , at least in biological terms , we are all .
&quot; What people will hopefully take away from this is that our genome does not care who we are and how open genetic data is for an interpretation and how subjective they are , &quot; said Dewey @-@ Hagborg , whose 2007 video work , Spurious Memories , is also on view .
&quot; DNA data can tell so many different stories , so that is 30 of these stories . &quot;
The last piece in the exhibition , which is kept in a separate room , is a page from the above @-@ mentioned comic novelle .
She shows Manning how she appears with a hands @-@ free phone in the hands of the Disciplinary Arrows of the United States such as King @-@ Kong .
&quot; When they turn your speech to a cold , they won , &quot; it is said .
&quot; So never stop the mouth . &quot;
&quot; It came out of a letter that she wrote to me , &quot; the artist said , who greets from Philadelphia .
&quot; I still get goose bumps when I talk about it . &quot;
Manning has not seen the exhibition in person yet - as it was anticipated in the comic book - but if it should , it will be greeted in the middle of the room by the masks , by their own genomic simulacras , which gaze like hordes of protesters .
This was also targeted , as everything in the exhibition , to the size ( 5 &quot; 2 , Manning &apos;s stature ) , where their genetic haplogroups were written with pencil .
&quot; In the past I had them on the wall , but I wanted them to feel like a crowd , &quot; said Dewey @-@ Hagborg from the portraits .
&quot; I wanted it to feel like a mass movement formed with Chelsea , like the movement that was also behind her liberation . &quot;
Professor of the University of Northwestern and an employee of the University of Oxford sought for killing .
A national @-@ wide hunt is in progress for a professor at Northwestern University and an employee at the University of Oxford , who is looking for suspicions after a man was killed in a luxurious apartment in Chicago .
On Monday , Wyndham Lathem ( 42 ) and Andrew Warren ( 56 ) received arrest warrants for murder for their alleged involvement in the death of Trenton H. James Cornell @-@ Duranleau , as the case law of the District Court of Cook County shows .
The documents say that Cornell @-@ Duranleau , 26 , died after several engravings .
A common warning system published by the Chicago Police Office states that the corpse was discovered on 27 July .
Officials received a call , arrived at the Grand Plaza Apartments and found the victim with several injuries to his body .
Cornell @-@ Duranleau was declared dead at the place of the events , and detectives investigate the engravings as murder , while the police is actively looking for the two suspects , officials said .
Lathem has been an extraordinary professor of microbiology and immunology at Northwestern University since 2007 .
