The startups that will help your company not to become the next user .
Amy Errett wanted to measure the luck of your employees in your e @-@ commerce start @-@ up , but the surveys did not work .
Answers were often vague , unhelpful or - worse - fraudulent .
And even if you promised anonymity , some workers didn &apos;t dare to go to the process .
&quot; There has simply never been consistency and objectivity , &quot; said Sarett , who operates the 75 person San Francisco e @-@ Commerce Hair Care Association Madison Reed .
She called people from the outside for help .
A new race of Human Resources startups is on the rise to help companies to find out how their employees feel .
By creating and licensing software that has the specific purpose of measuring employee commitment , it enables companies to conduct surveys , address specific groups and demographic groups , offer anonymity and complaint hotlines to employees , and in some cases whistleblowers allow to bypass the C @-@ Suite managers and directly contact the Board of Directors .
&quot; You now have tools like Strava and Fitbit for the pursuit of their health , but where is the fitbit for your company ? &quot; said Jim Barnett , Co @-@ Founder and Chief Executive of Redwood City Start @-@ up Glint , whose software analytics tools are used by companies to measure employee commitment .
Errett said that they won a better insight into what your employees think and feel in the three years with Glint .
In addition to the snap surveys and surveys of specific teams , it offers a heat map of the company that shows at a glance which units have the most complaints and which managers have low approval results - so that you can drill why .
Companies come to realize that they must remain at the top of the workplace culture , so that you do not become the next user involved in scandals since a former employee has published a blog post describing an environment of harassment in which those who have spoken were punished .
For startups like Glint , this desire for an overview is a lucrative business opportunity .
The global governance , regulation and compliance sector could have worth more than $ 118.7 billion in 2020 , according to the financial website Let &apos;s Talk Payments .
Denver , Colorado , Start @-@ up Convercent , which helps companies prevent and detect bad behavior , saw a growing interest and activity early this year in the midst of the Uber case in discredit .
Convercent has almost 600 clients , including Airbnb , Microsoft and Tesla .
Uber has recently signed up as a client .
Like Glint , Convercent lets companies send custom @-@ made &quot; impulse &quot; surveys , collect confidential answers in real time and view the heat maps of your problem areas .
It also offers an anonymous SMS hotline , which allows employees to report bad behavior .
And if the principal managing director is involved , complaints go directly to the Supervisory Board .
&quot; The public opinion court has taken over the regulators , &quot; said Patrick Quinlan , the founder and managing director of Convercent .
If a company is found that treats its employees badly or behaves unethically , it can experience costly consequences by consumer boycott , worker fluctuation and complaints , said Quinlan .
Ruby Tuesday , the restaurant chain with more than 25,000 employees and more than 500 locations , has employed Convercent for more than a year to ensure that employees are aware of the policy and procedures and have an easy way to reach their headquarters .
In the past , if an employee wanted to report a problem , he or she had to find a telephone number or email for the head office , submit a formal complaint and hope that he or she was taken seriously .
It was often an intimidation and inconvenient experience , said James Vitrano , Ruby Tuesdays General Counsel , who said there was no good way to track the complaints of employees .
Now that the company is using Convercent , problems that were previously hidden by executives in offices in cities or countries - such as discrimination , harassment or unfair internships - can be identified and addressed more quickly .
&quot; I can get closer to this holistic 360 degree view in the experience of employment , &quot; said Vitrano , who is in charge of the Ruby Tuesday Risk Management Group .
&quot; And we protect our shareholders from disputes over employment . &quot;
Companies began to take ethics , values and employee commitment more seriously in 2002 , after the bookkeeping company Arthur Andersen collapsed due to ethical violations by the Enron scandal , Quinlan said .
But it took the &quot; Social Media came to their right &quot; that companies realized that you could not prevent their dirty linen from going online .
&quot; Before using technology to monitor ethics , people used hope as a strategy , &quot; he said .
Both Glint and Convercent offer their software as a service , and encumber companies with recurrent fees for the use of their products .
It is a business model and an opportunity that is met with the consent of venture capital investors , both of whom have supported startups .
Convercent was awarded $ 10 million in the February financing by companies such as Sapphire Ventures and Tola Capital , raising the total capital to $ 47 million .
Glitzer secured $ 10 million in November from Bessemer Venture Partners and raised its total financing to $ 60 million .
These investments are hardly surprising given the networked nature of companies , culture and venture capital .
There is a growing research complex that shows that today &apos;s employees expect more from their jobs than they have done before .
In competitive markets such as Silicon Valley are high salaries and interesting projects only stakes .
The employees want to feel that they are accepted and appreciated and that they are giving your time to a company with a positive mission .
&quot; If people are happy to be in a company that is listening to their voices and that the work that you do is worthwhile , then they feel more committed to making this company a success , &quot; said Nina McQueen , Vice President of Global Benefits and Employee Experience at LinkedIn , which uses both Convercent and Glint .
Investors can achieve returns when their portfolio companies are in good working order , and companies are in good working when employees are engaged and committed .
If the analytics tools of third parties promise to increase the engagement and the commitment of the employees , it is no surprise that you will find support .
The data on the commitment of the employees according to the experts of the workplace culture is important .
But the data is useless if the company &apos;s managers do not take any measures .
Indeed , if a company asks employees for your feedback , it may have an expectation that change is on its way .
And if the change is not coming quickly enough , or not at all , this can breed disappointment and discourage employees .
&quot; If you ask for 4,000 suggestions , you must be prepared to have 4,000 talks , &quot; said Russell Raath , President of Consulting at the Kotter International Business Management Company , who has seen companies that have too much rely on the gathering of data .
&quot; If you don &apos;t follow , your colleagues will ask , &quot; Have you really heard me ?
Do you care about it ?
And who you are not concerned , why should I do it ? &quot;
Madison Reed , who is now conducting monthly employee surveys , was able to initiate measures the same day if a problem is reported about Glint , so Salvation said .
And , after gathering feedback from frustrated employees , the speedy decision @-@ making process and the quality of communication , she was able to reorganise several teams within the company and add a communication training to address the problems .
At Ruby Tuesday , the company is getting more and more insights about its employees and it hopes that in the long run , this will lead to a better retention of employees in a sector known for its high sales .
&quot; If you are not obliged to create a culture of transparency , you will lose people , &quot; said Vitrano .
&quot; And if you lose people , you also lose customers .
And if you lose customers , you will lose companies .
The NCAA removes the two @-@ day practices and makes a long day for the Bruins .
