Facebook intends to intensify factual verification in the fight against fake news .
Facebook wants to send more potential articles with false reports to third @-@ party factresses and then display their results under the original post , said the world &apos;s largest online social network on Thursday .
The company said in a statement on its website that it will begin by using updated machine learning methods to detect possible false reports and send them to factual verifiers who may display results of the factual check from the original article .
Facebook was criticized as one of the main distribution points for so @-@ called fake news , many of whom believe they also influenced the U.S. presidential elections in 2016 .
The issue has also become a major political issue in Europe , with the French voters flooded with false stories in the run @-@ up to the May presidential elections , and Germany supports a plan to punish social media networks if they fail to eliminate hate @-@ filled postings as before the September elections .
On Thursday , Facebook said in a separate German statement that a test of the new facts review will be launched in the U.S. , France , the Netherlands and Germany .
&quot; In addition to the realisation of what stories are being challenged by factual auditors from third parties , people want more context to make informed decisions about what they read and share , &quot; said Sara Su , product manager for Facebook messages in a blog .
She added that Facebook will continue to test its feature to the &quot; related article &quot; and is working on other changes to its message feed to reduce false messages .
Creating a comic book : A castle in the history of England .
In his recent past , the little Scotney Castle was seen in a squeeze music video and was a loophole for Margaret Thatcher in the 1970 &apos;s and 1980 &apos;s .
But Scotney has witnessed 700 years of turbulent history from his cozy seat in the Bewl River Valley in Kent and has now become the inspiration for a graphic novel by Jamie Rhodes , a mophaired Yorkshireman with a preference for historical documents .
&quot; I like looking at the handwriting of someone who has been dead for 300 years , &quot; he says with bravery .
&quot; What a personal relationship to hold this letter in my hand . &quot;
&apos;A Castle in England &apos; covers the Middle Ages to the Edward Age and documents the families who lived in Scotney , each story being illustrated by another emerging British artist - Isaac Lenkiewicz , Briony May Smith , William Exley , Becky Palmer and Isabel Greenberg .
It is not the first book by Rhodes based on an archive : He wrote &apos; Dead Men &apos;s Teeth and Other Stories &apos; in 2014 , a collection inspired by documents in the British Library - a project that he found unexpectedly emotional .
&quot; I only responded to the thumb print on the side of a letter written by a double agent in the 1700 &apos;s , who worked for the Jakobiner , &quot; he says .
&quot; That &apos;s his thumb !
And letters always smell of smoke , as at that time a fire was lit for the light .
For someone who lives 300 years later , the smell has an effect as if he were there .
Oh , I love archives ! &quot;
For someone who is so sacked with old papers that he is supported by the National Trust and the Arts Council England and who lived for almost four months in a castle ( &quot; To be honest , I &apos;ve overrated my welcome &quot; ) , this all seems to be too good to be true .
Rhodes spent his days exploring the Victorian &quot; new &quot; castle , browsing the archives and studying the many treasures and curiosities of the castle , such as the hydraulic heads mounted on the walls and the bottles that had spent centuries in the trench .
He walked through the terrain and the ruins of the old castle and talked to landscape managers , gardeners and sometimes also artists who worked on the illustrations of his book .
Then , at night , he drank &quot; whiskey and began to write . &quot;
The intimate story of Scotney is relatively unknown , as the National Trust only received full access in 2006 when the last heir , Elizabeth Hussey , died .
When Rhodes arrived a decade later , the employees just began to take care of the archive , which he described as &quot; hundreds of years in which aristocrats said &quot; Oh , throw it on the attic . &quot;
Diaries , letters from the time of the English Empires , maps that show who possessed what : &quot; You could think that conflict on the land would be boring , &quot; says Rhodes , &quot; but there was a birch to fight for the two families .
Young guys , let &apos;s go ! &quot;
Some of the stories in the collection use the castle as a starting point to explore the history of the English on a wider scale , including The Labourer , which follows a man leaving the newly built castle to join the 1381 Farmers &quot; uprising .
&quot; There is a record of a turmoil that appeared in the castle in 1380 in this book entitled &apos; Knightly Families of Kent and Sussex &apos; , &quot; says Rhodes .
&quot; The farmer &apos;s uprising began in this area and I thought that a revolt was a good start .
Maybe the two are connected ? &quot;
Other stories are deeply rooted in the history of the castle - such as &apos; The Priest &apos; , which tells the story of the Jesuit patron that the Darrell family hid for seven years during the British Reformation .
Or &apos; The Smuggler &apos; , which depicts the 18th century smuggling Arthur Darrell , staging his own funeral by filling a coffin with stones ( a discovery that was made only years later when his coffin was excavated ) .
The Darrells are Rhodes &apos; favourite family of Scotney : &quot; The gentlemen seemed to be quite a roar , repeatedly making debts and spending money on things they could not afford .
Other generations of Darrell men solved their debt crises by marrying rich old women - I imagine them as a bunch of good looking boys , with a talent to advertise Erinnen . &quot;
After finishing his work in the castle , Rhodes is looking for other unique experiences in writing .
He has spent two weeks in the Finnish wilderness for a comic novel about Elämänmäki , the site of a sanatorium from the early 20th century , which has been recovered by nature since then .
But Scotney has a special place for him .
&quot; I was sad to go back , &quot; he says .
It was like this : &quot; Ah , now I have to finish the damn life again . &quot;
