Military reforms in Turkey - Grossgans fear of the army .
Every year , in early August , the High Military Council ( YAS ) in Ankara advises on the retirement and promotion of the officers in the corps .
So this week too .
At first glance a normal process .
But nothing is routine in the Turkish armed forces one year after the coup attempt .
The meeting was no longer held in the General Staff building , as it was in the past , but in the Prime Minister &apos;s seat .
The five Vice Premier Premiers as well as the Minister of Defence , the Interior , the Justice and the Foreign Affairs , as well as the Minister of Foreign Affairs , were now also present at the meetings , which was chaired by the Prime Minister .
The majority of the YAS &apos; s civilians were in the majority .
The government is retaining the reins in the military .
Hulusi Akar , Chief of Staff , remains in office , but the commanders of the army , the air forces and the navy were disposed .
Since the failed coup , the head of state , Mr R. R. Ozgan 169 , of the 326 generals and admirals , has fired .
More than 10,000 soldiers were removed from the ranks of the army - mostly due to alleged links to the movement of the exiled preacher Fethullah Gülen , whom the Lord sees as the mover of the coup attempt .
486 people are expected to be involved in the coup attempt and have been the subject of negotiations in Ankara since this week .
Already the day after the coup , the first thing that was termed by the name of the change , was that it was a &quot; gift of God , &quot; as it gave him the opportunity to &quot; clean the armed forces completely . &quot;
But has it really been the army that is in the grip of the earth ?
At the end of February , the newspaper Hürriyet published an article entitled &quot; Uncomfortable in the Military Headquarter . &quot;
The general public is concerned about the increasing &quot; Islamization &quot; of the army , which is manifested , among other things , in the lifting of the ban on the headscarf for female soldiers , wrote the newspaper .
The large @-@ scale cleaning of the army and the security forces will continue .
In mid @-@ July , by decree , Erdgan had another 7395 government servants to light , including 2303 policemen and 546 soldiers .
It seemed that the military was under the control of the Reigno .
The generals were formerly the actual lords of the country .
They three times plunged on the basis of their role as guardians of the secular order of the state .
After his 2002 election victory , the power of the military was gradually pushing back , not least in the wake of the reform guidelines of the European Union .
Former officers say that Erdgan is driving forward the Islamization of the armed forces in a targeted way .
In the magazine &quot; Vocal Europe , &quot; a nameless officer , who was looking for asylum after the coup for fear of persecution abroad , warned that the Turkish army would be &quot; full of extremists and salafists &quot; in a few years .
If this is the case , such a development could not leave the NATO cold .
In the wake of the cleansing , the Alliance is in doubt about the Turkish army &apos;s readiness to use the aircraft .
The leadership structures and operational skills are &quot; weakened , &quot; says Nato diplomats .
