Red meat , dairy products and chocolate could help alleviate the inflammatory disease ( IBD ) .
Red meat and chocolate can sound like a nightmare for your digestion system .
But a new study asserts the exact opposite : a diet rich in meat @-@ based proteins and dairy products could relieve the symptoms of inflammatory bowel disease patients .
The incurable condition , which affects about 1.3 million in the USA and 300,000 in the UK , causes gastric cramps and fissures , recurring or bleeding diarrhoea , weight loss and extreme fatigue .
While the patients are usually advised to avoid meat and dairy products in order to relieve their symptoms , researchers at the Washington University have found that the essential amino acid tryptophan of proteins helps to develop immune cells that promote a tolerant intestine .
The chief researcher , Dr. Marco Colonna , explained that immune cells are patroling in the intestine to ensure that harmful microbes that hide in the food do not penetrate the body .
Cells that are able to trigger inflammation are compensated by cells that promote tolerance and protect the body without damaging sensitive tissue .
If the balance tends to be too far towards inflammation , this can result in an inflammatory disease .
Experiments have found that a kind of tolerance @-@ promoting immune cells occur in mice that carries a certain bacterium in their infants .
And the bacterium needs Tryptophan - one of the building blocks of proteins - to trigger the cell appearance .
Professor Colonna said : &quot; We have made a connection between a bacteria type - Lactobacillus reuteri - which is a normal part of the microbiome and the development of a population of cells that promotes tolerance .
The more Tryptophan the mice had in their food , the more they had of these immune cells . &quot;
He proposed that the combination of L. reuteri and a diet rich in Tryptophan should promote a more tolerant , less inflammatory intestine , if the same system works in humans .
The postdoctoral doctor Dr. Luisa Cervantes @-@ Barragan investigated a kind of immune cell that promotes tolerance when she found out that a group of study mice had such cells , but another group of the same tribe , which was however housed separately , had not .
She assumed that the difference had to do with the microbiomes of the mice - the community of bacteria , viruses and mushrooms that normally live in the tract of the stomach .
The mice &apos;s arms were sequenced to DNA and six types of bacteria were found , which were present in the mice with the immune cells , but without them were missing in the mice .
Mice was raised in a sterile environment , so that they lacked a microbiom and they did not develop this type of immune cells , and they were given the L. reuteri and the immune cells .
To understand how the bacteria had an impact on the immune system , the researchers let L. reuteri grow in a liquid and then transferred small amounts of the liquid - without the bacteria - to immature immune cells , which were isolated from the mice .
The immune cells developed to the tolerant cells .
When the active component was purified from the liquid , it proved to be a by @-@ product of the tryptophan metabolism known as indol @-@ 3 lactic acid .
When researchers doubled the amount of tryptophan in the mice lining , the number of such cells increased by about 50 percent , but when the tryptophanous values were halved , the number of cells decreased by half .
Dr. Cervantes @-@ Barragan explained that people have the same tolerant cells as mice , and that most of us L. reuteri protect in our stomach @-@ intestine tracts .
It is not known whether tryptophan by @-@ products from L. reuteri develop the cells in humans as if they were in mice , but defects in genes in relation to Tryptophan were found in people with inflammatory bowel disease .
She said , &quot; The development of these cells is probably something we want to promote , as these cells control inflammation on the inner surface of the intestine .
Potential , high tryptophanous values in the presence of L. reuteri can lead to an expansion of this population . &quot;
