The White House &apos;s advertising costs cost the tax payer thousands of euros .
The White House decision to fly a naval helicopter to South Lawn for an event that emphasizes the American production in the last month , costs the tax payer up to 24,000 dollars , according to the military &apos;s data , handed over to the TIME magazine .
The green and white Sikorsky VH @-@ 3D , known as Marine One , when the president is on board , was the heart of the event on 17th July in the White House with American construction programs .
President Donald Sturm , Vice President Mike Pence and White House leaders made a tour of the products of all 50 countries .
The Connecticut helicopter was manufactured in addition to a Maine yacht , a Wisconsin Fire car and a Mississippi fork .
The White House Military Office requested the presence of the helicopter in a fragmentary order or FRAGO , barely 36 hours before the helicopter landed on the South Lawn , after the records of the marine Helicopter Squadron One ( HMX @-@ 1 ) , the unit responsible for the operation of the helicopter .
&quot; I just wanted you to be aware of this because it is such an unusual event with high visibility , &quot; the HMX @-@ 1 commander wrote on the eve of the event to his superior , the Deputy naval commander for aviation .
The news showed that the helicopter arrived on the South Lawn at 7am on 17th July for the event at 3pm and stayed until the lawn was cleared between 6pm and 9pm that evening .
The note adds that , should the departure from the White House be delayed , a second flight crew would be required for the return flight , due to the limitation of the service hours .
The original crew was on the helicopter during the event , while the security staff of the unit remained on the ground for a while .
The records made available to the TIME did not clearly show , if a second flight crew was eventually required .
The Hatch Act prohibits federal employees from using state property for political purposes .
Data records indicate that HMX @-@ 1 asked if the event could violate the regulation .
&quot; One question we asked was whether it was a violation of the Hatch Act and we were informed that this was not the case , &quot; wrote the commander .
&quot; Sounds as if you &apos;re locked , &quot; the Deputy Commander replied .
The event &quot; Made in America &quot; was referred to as an official event by the White House and would not have been covered by the Hatch Act .
But also official events have political overtones .
At the event , the President gave impetus to the reform of the health care reform that was in progress in the Senate and he propagated the efforts to curb the government &apos;s regulations .
The use of the aircraft , known as Marine One or Air Force One , for politically advantageous purposes is hardly a new phenomenon .
The presidents are obliged to use them for the trip .
But to ask for their use only for a photo @-@ op is unusual .
In February , the first presidential aircraft to perform taxi services in Melbourne , Florida , was the subject of a study by the president , and his Twitter account is full of reports complaining about the use of the Presidential Aircraft by his predecessor .
&quot; The White House is excited about the show &quot; Made In America &quot; - the products from all 50 countries show that are made by American workers with American materials , &quot; said the Deputy Secretary of the White House , Lindsay Walters .
HMX @-@ 1 crews often carry out training flights in the Washington and South Lawn area to dispose of the U.S. President in the White House with precise centimeter substitutions .
When the G20 summit in Germany in early July , when the country was occupied by the country &apos;s population , HMX @-@ 1 carried out 58 exercises in the White House .
The flight for the &quot; Made in America &quot; event counted the flight crew for the month in the training needs , a civil servant said .
Helicopter units flew an additional 11 hours on that day for &quot; Non @-@ Presidential Support Missions . &quot;
The White House said that the tax payers did not take the burden of any of the other props presented at the event .
A Sikorsky speaker said that the company was not involved in the exposition of the helicopter on the South Lawn .
HMX @-@ 1 does not have a budget for the Presidential flights , and it is difficult to determine the exact costs of the event .
However , the DoD is charged at a rate of 24.380 USD per hour for the VH @-@ 3D , as the US Defense Department &apos;s hourly rates for the 2017 financial year .
The unit records the aircraft &apos;s flight time as a 30 @-@ minute flight from the home base of the Marine Corps Air Facility in Quantico in Virginia .
The White House argues that the actual costs of operating the helicopter are far below this rate .
&quot; To set the costs for the tax payers based on the estimations for the refund rates is highly misleading , &quot; said Walters .
&quot; These estimates include personnel , maintenance and many other hidden costs , which are included in the annual funds . &quot;
According to a RAND study from 2015 , the reimbursement rates for DoD do not include the personnel costs , but take the costs of fuel , maintenance and contract into account .
At that time , the former White House press spokesman , Sean Spicer , defended the use of the helicopter for the event .
&quot; The idea is to present this week things that are made in America , &quot; Spicer said .
&quot; And I know that Sikorsky and the state of Connecticut are very proud that they contribute to our national security , and I expect that hundreds , if not thousands , of people depend on it .
And I think , like most Americans , we are all proud of the helicopter and other military equipment , for which so many Americans have worked tirelessly .
It is of course appropriate to emphasize this . &quot;
It was not clear if he interacted with the helicopter as he entered the tour of the South Lawn for the event , but he sat in the driver &apos;s seat of a fire car .
In the White House , the speaker spoke to Sikorsky representatives and joked with the media about his own fleet of company products .
&quot; I know Sikorsky very well , &quot; the President said , &quot; I have three of them . &quot;
