Afghan democracy is dependent on the political role of women , the UN says .
Elections can only be representative if all members of the society , including women , play a role , the head of the UN mission in Afghanistan said on Wednesday .
Nicholas Haysom , Acting Head of the United Nations Mission , said that hundreds of women will be standing in the provincial council elections , three women are nominees for the role of Vice President and more than 30 percent of newly registered voters are women .
&quot; The role of women can not be overestimated , &quot; he said on Wednesday in a statement .
Elections can only be truly representative and credible if women participate without restrictions and are included in all parts of the voting process .
Afghan elections for the provincial council and the presidential elections will take place on 5th April .
The Taliban have published threats against an election process that coincide with the transition from international combat to a consultative role .
This year , the elections will be transferred for the first time peacefully from a democratically elected government to the next .
Haysom said that a strong role for women will further enhance Afghanistan &apos;s democratic success .
The Afghan Independent Electoral Commission reports that 1.2 million women are registered by a total of 3.6 million registered voters .
