Interview : &quot; Domspatzen report relieves Pope @-@ Brother Georg Ratzinger &quot; .
The Regensburg Domspatzen report on the abuses relieves Pope @-@ brother Georg Ratzinger of the burden of the abuse .
This is what the author and historian Michael Hesemann says .
The final report on the abuse scandal at the Regensburg domspatzen is still a source of discussion .
Christian Weisnder , speaker of the reform movement &quot; We are church , &quot; explained after publication to the German press agency , saying that he expects the former Regensburg bishop and today &apos;s Cardinal Gerhard Ludwig Müller as well as the master mayor Georg Ratzinger , the 93 @-@ year @-@ old brother of the emprisoned Pope Benedict XVI , to excuse the church .
It would be of great benefit to the Catholic Church to have Müller and Ratzinger express their deep regret about their own omissions or their misjudgement of the events at that time .
The Vatican newspaper Osservatore Romano , on the other hand , criticized the fact that the dominion is subjected to &quot; two standards &quot; in reporting violence .
Regarding the attacks in Regensburg , nobody doubts that these are &quot; shameful and shameful acts &quot; that would have to be punished and prevented in the future ; but the &quot; degree of media manipulation &quot; is notable , according to the author Lucetta Scaraffia , a historian and member of the Italian Bioethics Committee .
&quot; The habit of calling the Catholic Church the source of all evils is already part of the everyday life and prepares public opinion to consider it to be a normal source , &quot; writes Scaraffia .
Prince Gloria von Thurn and Taxis also said in an interview with the Bayerische Rundfunk : &quot; You like to go to the church and that &apos;s a found feast . &quot;
She also pointed out that in her youth &quot; blows &quot; were a normal educational means &quot; to deal with the good children I was . &quot;
They would find it unfair to apply the standards of today to earlier decades , as the world has changed .
In connection with the reports of abuse cases , Pope @-@ brother Georg Ratzinger also appeared .
The author and historian Michael Hesemann , who wrote the book of memory &quot; My brother the Pope &quot; with Ratzinger ( translated into 12 languages ) is quite right .
&quot; I must protect Georg Ratzinger , &quot; says Hesemann , &quot; because all this has nothing to do with him , &quot; he evaluates the results of the final report .
What exactly is in the 440 @-@ page report that the attorney @-@ at @-@ law Ulrich Weber , who was commissioned by the Bistum Regensburg , has submitted with the reconnaissance ?
A total of 547 domspatts in Regensburg were sexually abused between 1945 and the beginning of the 1990s &quot; with high plausibility . &quot;
Georg Ratzinger , who was active in Regensburg from 1964 to 1994 , wrote in the final report : &quot; In summary , it can be noted that in many cases forms of physical and mental violence were used by Domkapellmeister R. .
Especially with a high degree of emotionality , the punishment for the students could be more severe .
Nevertheless , in contrast to many other people accused , it is apparent that many of the victims value the general humanity of R. and therefore in many cases even associate positive memories with him despite the violence . &quot;
In the interview , Hesemann explains why the final report on the abuse scandal at the Regensburg domspatts relieves Georg Ratzinger of his opinion .
Mr. Hesemann , the 440 @-@ page report on the &quot; Incidents of Violence on Patented Orders in the Regensburg Domspatzen &quot; gives a contradictory picture of the master of the Regensburg .
On the one hand , many students emphasize his &quot; humanity . &quot;
On the other hand , he also appears as a cholerician and perfectionist , who has often become tangible when a domain set has not achieved top performance in music .
How do you rate this final report as your biographer ?
Michael Hesemann : If you take the trouble to read the 440 @-@ page report from the front to the rear , you should rather ask yourself why the name Georg Ratzinger is found in it .
He is only 8 pages and is clearly exempted from any suspicion of complicity or guilt in the terrible cases of sexual abuse .
The excess of violence at the pre @-@ school also occurred outside its sphere of influence .
You can at best blame him for not having learned of it too late or for not having taken the first hints seriously enough .
The Domspatzengymnasium , at which he was working , was perceived by practically all as - literally - &quot; redemption , &quot; &quot; heaven &quot; and &quot; better world . &quot;
The majority of his pupils Georg Ratzinger is an excellent witness , describing him as &quot; warm , &quot; &quot; popular , &quot; &quot; sincere , &quot; &quot; competent &quot; and &quot; understanding , &quot; as an extremely good @-@ natured teacher , who provided the guys regularly with cake , biscuits and candy and was &quot; always surrounded by grapes of children . &quot;
Only a minority complained about his perfectionism and his high emotion , which has also led to the outbreak of anger .
He has admitted , deeply regretted and apologized that he didn &apos;t just crack with chairs and throwing the key dog on the floor , but also distributed the earphones and pulled all too cheerful guys on the hair or the ears .
However , to his salvation , it must be said that such methods of education were widespread in the period in question , the 1960s and the 1970s , both in parents and teachers .
Do you know such methods of breastfeeding ?
Of course , I think each of us born before 1968 .
I also had the opportunity to experience this in a secular school , a state @-@ run high school .
Compared to what this report is actually about , namely the horrible sexual abuse and the excesses of violence at the preschool , these are , of course , small atelles .
One wonders if it is not more about his renown and his role as brother of Benedict XVI that many media are rushing to him .
Many of you who have never understood him are still working on the Bavarian Pope .
The truth is : the report relieves Georg Ratzinger of all aspects !
In the media a lot was reported about the topic of abuse in the Regensburger Domspatzen in connection with the report .
Will Georg Ratzinger be accused of any involvement or cognition in the sexual abuse in the final report ?
Michael Hesemann : No .
Not one of the 67 victims of sexual assaults claim to have entrusted themselves to the master of the Cathedral .
Not even the parents were dedicated .
&quot; I have never dared to talk to my father or mother about it , &quot; the person concerned said , representing the others , &quot; It was all embarrassing to me . &quot;
The true problem in the education of sexual abuse was and is that most victims remain silent out of shame .
If they didn &apos;t even trust parents and sibling , then certainly not a choral leader , who was a sort of a professional colleague of the perpetrators .
How do the Domspatzen pupils rate Renter &apos;s role in terms of abuse ?
Michael Hesemann : A student surveyed said : &quot; Regarding sexual abuse ... I can see ... no case in which he ( Georg Ratzinger ) would have had some insights in time and could have stopped the respective perpetrator .
I think it &apos;s really absurd to suppose that it &apos;s the guy who is the one to be ... boys have more penetrating , clearer or more open than to their parents , boarding ladders , trust teachers , etc .
That you talk to him about sex ... - unthinkable . &quot;
The report comes to the conclusion that &quot; the fact that the pupils and / or their surroundings entrusted themselves to him in the run @-@ up to the project could not be confirmed in the course of the investigation . &quot;
As far as sexual abuse is concerned , Georg Ratzinger is to be exempted from any community .
This means that he cannot be accused of not having prevented or brought him to the attention of the public .
Therefore , there is no reason to associate his good name with these heinous crimes , as unfortunately happened in some cases .
A lot of the media has read about &quot; violence addictions &quot; at the Regensburg domspatts .
The former master of the Cathedral , Georg Ratzinger , is accused in the report of &quot; Waying Out . &quot;
Is it necessary for him to live with this accusation ?
Michael Hesemann : That &apos;s the big problem that everything was mixed and mixed in the reporting to a huge melange of sexual abuse and violence and somewhere in the middle of it , Georg Ratzinger .
But that is , in turn , an abuse of the report .
The difference is that the pre @-@ school and the high school of the Domspatts are cleanly separated , between sexual abuse and violence and , let &apos;s say , other accusations .
In addition , the report deals with events from 1945 onwards , including four decades in which Georg Ratzinger was not the choral director of the Domspatzen .
The whole terrible teaching of the violins , the cases in which boys were subjected to a real bullying and maltreatment by sadistic teachers and suffered terribly from it , all happened at the Preschool in Etterzhausen and Pielenhofen , where scandalous conditions had to reign .
But Domkapellmeister Georg Ratzinger had nothing to do with them , as he only saw the boys when they were at the Domspatzengymnasium .
On that , as most of the witnesses emphasize , the topic &quot; Preschool &quot; was taboo .
One was only happy to escape from it and enjoyed the much better treatment and for many even &quot; heavenly &quot; conditions .
What was the difference at the Domspatzengymnasium ?
Michael Hesemann : Although there was already discipline , there was no sadistic violence .
And this also applies to Georg Ratzinger , in which the report concludes that &quot; many victims value the general humanity of R. and therefore in many cases even associate positive memories with it despite violence . &quot;
At the most , the accusation of having looked the other way round is still going to remain .
However , despite all efforts , the report could only define two periods in which it was told of the conditions at the pre @-@ school .
The first time in 1970 / 71 , when he just began to establish himself , and didn &apos;t want to hear of the events at a school where he was not active and responsible ( the witness says ) , and then again around 1993 .
Did Georg Ratzinger shut his ears to the state of the art ?
Michael Hesemann : No , as early as 1989 , he wrote a letter to the director of the Domspatzengymnasium , which the report even reproduces .
He points out to the fact that &quot; the preschool continues to practise the caning in the future . &quot;
He already recommended urgent intervention in view of the risk of negative press releases .
This was of course at a time when legal clarity was in place , when caning was long ago also forbidden in Bavaria .
Before that they took place in a legal grey zone , as everyone of us still knows .
It was also hard for him to estimate , where the people concerned might have been exaggerated or dramatized , and where the permissible limit was really exceeded .
But it is also impossible to say that he has always looked the other way .
That &apos;s just not true .
Only in 1980 was the caning of schools officially abolished in Bavaria .
Prince Gloria of Thurn and Taxis asserted that it was indeed unfair to apply the present educational standards to the time .
How do you see this ?
Michael Hesemann : We historians talk about the &quot; pitfall of anachronism , &quot; i.e. the danger of judging the past by today &apos;s moral standards .
The Prince is already right .
But this does not of course apply to sexual abuse , which was abominable at all times , and also to what was violated in the preschool with resistance and violence on innocent child souls , which goes far beyond the usual one .
That &apos;s all horrible and I &apos;m glad that it is finally being worked out .
But at the same time I have to protect Georg Ratzinger , for all this had nothing to do with him .
His means of discipline and punishment , i.e. , earphones and pulling on the hair and ears , were widespread in the 1970ies .
When physical punishment was then forbidden , he adhered strictly to it .
What image of his rebellious period did Georg Ratzinger have in the talks with you for the book &quot; My Brother the Pope &quot; ?
Are there any questions about caning punishment ?
Michael Hesemann : Well , the book is about the life of his brother Joseph , that is , Pope Benedict XVI , told from his perspective , as the Domspatts only play a supporting role .
Nevertheless , I don &apos;t conceal the fact that he was in charge of a strict regime .
But this , too , has to be said : Without this perfectionism , without the challenge of unconditional discipline , he would have been unable to form an institution of world @-@ class from the nationally known Regensburg domspatts , which were the true European cultural ambassadors and their musical tradition , which went on tour twice in the USA ( 1983 and 1987 ) and Japan ( 1988 and 1991 ) .
With biscuits and candy alone , you won &apos;t be making big singers out of disgusting boys and you won &apos;t be standing before any success , no matter what field , but always discipline , passion and self @-@ supreme .
What kind of person did you see Georg Ratzinger talk about in the book ?
Michael Hesemann : Through my work on the book and beyond , I really got to know Georg Ratzinger very well and have seen him as a wonderful , humorous , benevolent , deeply faith and good people , from whom it is impossible to imagine that he could do something for a fly .
I get to know how many former dominion patches still today , 23 years after his embellishment , keep regular contact to him , visit him , read him out - he is almost blind - and treat him like a member of her family .
Those who have not forgiven him to this day that he was once a strict one to them are clearly in the minority .
Today , most of the Domspatzen are thankful for the fact that he has enriched their lives and brought the world of music closer to them .
And that he was always a good , paternal teacher - a severe father , of course , that may be - but also a worthy respectful person , to whom they still look up and to whom they almost worship .
And please let &apos;s be very clear : all that is being accused of him are methods of education that were the order of the day .
We can be glad that we have long since overcome this time .
But it is highly unjust to turn a man into a media scapegoat , just because he is prominent and has an even more prominent brother .
The evil intent behind it is only too obvious .
The report that has just been published relieves him of the strain .
Now the decency would dictate to let a 93 @-@ year @-@ old man who has earned himself a great deal of merit finally in peace without the outside of Bavaria and perhaps still Germany , no one would know the Regensburg domspatzen .
